on': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mueller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4042ee1f-61f7-5661-8c3e-d893e1a422d3', 'word_count': 789, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4042ee1f-61f7-5661-8c3e-d893e1a422d3'}, {'abstract': 'After the Bulls built a big lead, the Knicks managed to nose ahead in the second half, only to go 2 of 19 from the field in the final period.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/02/sports/basketball/knicks-search-for-normalcy-after-robberies-of-cleanthony-early-and-derrick-williams.html', 'snippet': 'After the Bulls built a big lead, the Knicks managed to nose ahead in the second half, only to go 2 of 19 from the field in the final period.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — Coach Derek Fisher allowed 2 minutes 3 seconds to elapse before he motioned to an official for relief. Fisher’s first timeout on Friday evening was a needed reprieve for the Knicks, who gathered near the bench and enjoyed a brief stretch during which the Bulls were prohibited from driving for layups, sinking 3-pointers and causing general mayhem.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/02/sports/02KNICKS3/02KNICKS-1451704826992-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/02/sports/02KNICKS3/02KNICKS-1451704826992-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/02/sports/02KNICKS3/02KNICKS-1451704826992-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'c