 has become a remarkably productive laboratory of criminal justice reform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day last month, Dannel Malloy, the governor of Connecticut, was sitting with a small group of inmates at a New Haven jail, where he had gone to announce a new job-training program for prisoners nearing their release date.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/04/opinion/04mon1/04mon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 744, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/04/opinion/04mon1/04mon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 744}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/04/opinion/04mon1/04mon1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 826, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/04/opinion/04mon1/04mon1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 967, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/04/opinion/04mon1/04mon1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/04/opinion/04mon1/04mon1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/04/opinion/04mon1/04mon1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Connecticut’s Second-Chance Society', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Connecticut’s Second-Chance Society', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malloy, Dannel P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Probation and Parole', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-04T08:20:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d31a812e-0dbb-56f6-8d00-8d02fe95ef99', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d31a812e-0dbb-56f6-8d00-8d02fe95ef99'}, {'abstract': 'Other politicians remade the rules right in front of our eyes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/04/opinion/campaign-stops/trump-did-not-break-politics.html', 'snippet': 'Other politicians remade the rules right in front of our eyes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Politics, we’re often told, is governed by rules — basic principles that explain not just what’s going to happen, but what rational candidates and parties can be expected to do. Donald J. Trump seems to casually defy all these rules — insulting Senator John McCain, a veteran; disparaging women; and building a rhetorical wall to keep out the single demographic group that his party seems to need for its survival, Latinos.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/04/opinion/04schmittWeb/04schmittWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/20