'Franklin Roosevelt’s White House feared, threatened and shunned The Chicago Defender for calling out its racism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/the-defender-by-ethan-michaeli.html', 'snippet': 'Franklin Roosevelt’s White House feared, threatened and shunned The Chicago Defender for calling out its racism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Franklin Roosevelt held an average of 84 presidential news conferences a year — 14 times the number given by Ronald Reagan and three to four times the output of Bill Clinton, George W. Bush or Barack Obama. Roosevelt charmed the White House press corps to within an inch of its life, leaked big stories to favored reporters and still made time for writers from obscure trade journals and others who were technically ineligible for press credentials. He nevertheless shunned the Negro press, shutting it out of the White House press corps until the last of his 12 years in office. By avoiding fire-breathing newspapers like The Chicago Defender, The Baltimore Afro-American and The Pittsburgh Courier, Roosevelt insulated himself from questions about what \xadAfrican-Americans saw as the burning issue of the 1940s: the government’s decision to embrace segregation in the military. Separating military men and women — and even the plasma in the wartime blood bank — by race, the government ratified racial apartheid in the South and introduced Jim Crow segregation into parts of the country where it had been unknown.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10STAPLES/10STAPLES-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 921, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10STAPLES/10STAPLES-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 921}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10STAPLES/10STAPLES-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 668, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10STAPLES/10STAPLES-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1567, 'width': 1021, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10STAPLES/10STAPLES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10STAPLES/10STAPLES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10STAPLES/10STAPLES-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Defender,’ by Ethan Michaeli', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A ‘Most Dangerous’ Newspaper', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Staples, Brent', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Defender', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michaeli, Ethan (1967- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Defender: How the Legendary Black Newspaper Changed America From the Age of the Pullman Porters to the Age of Obama (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-04T10:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brent Staples', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brent', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Staples', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1dffba6-7e51-5c89-9854-0df04a647314', 'word_count': 1381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1dffba6-7e51-5c89-9854-0df04a647314'}, {'abstract': 'Strout’s new novel is about a girl’s unquestioning, almost animal love for her mother, and her mother’s love in return.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/elizabeth-strouts-my-name-is-lucy-barton.html', 'snippet': 'Strout’s new novel is about a girl’s unquestioning, almost animal love for her mother, and her mother’s love in return.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of this nation’s most abiding myths is that social origins don’t matter. Each of us is Gatsby, or can be, with the potential to be reinvented and obliterate the past. This is nowhere more true than in New York City, where, surrounded by millions, each person supposedly stands upon his or her own merits. If we reach a sophisticated urban consensus on how to speak, how to dress, how to live, then who will know what lies beneath the surface? Who will know what any one of us might really mean by words like “home,” “childhood” or “love”?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-CoverMessud/0110-BKS-CoverMessud-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-CoverMessud/0110-BKS-CoverMessud-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 652}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-CoverMessud/0110-BKS-CoverMessud-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 943, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-CoverMessud/0110-BKS-CoverMessud-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1885, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-CoverMessud/0110-BKS-CoverMessud-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-CoverMessud/0110-BKS-CoverMessud-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-CoverMessud/0110-BKS-CoverMessud-thumbLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Elizabeth Strout’s ‘My Name Is Lucy Barton’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Sound of Silence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strout, Elizabeth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'My Name Is Lucy Barton (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-04T10:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Messud', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Messud', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://ar