e History', 'kicker': 'Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Spirals Fly, Records Keep Falling ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Records and Achievements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-04T20:06:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chase Stuart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chase', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stuart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/324873da-9873-5a0d-8e51-4a0b4ad17409', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/324873da-9873-5a0d-8e51-4a0b4ad17409'}, {'abstract': 'This series, based on the second book of the fantasy trilogy by Terry Brooks, features characters desperately trying to save a magical tree that protects the world from demons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/05/arts/television/tv-review-mtv-shannara-chronicles.html', 'snippet': 'This series, based on the second book of the fantasy trilogy by Terry Brooks, features characters desperately trying to save a magical tree that protects the world from demons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Terry Brooks’s original “Shannara” trilogy predates much of the name-brand fantasy fiction that has been turned into box-office gold lately. You need to keep reminding yourself of that as you’re watching “The Shannara Chronicles,” MTV’s television adaptation, which begins on Tuesday night. Amberle and Eretria, the saga’s two strongest female characters, might look like Katniss Everdeen knockoffs, but chronologically, Katniss could be either’s daughter.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 