o would upset their longtime rivals in Iran. In fact, the royal court in Riyadh was probably counting on it. It got what it wanted. The deterioration of relations has been precipitous: Protesters in Tehran sacked Saudi Arabia’s embassy; in retaliation, Saudi Arabia severed diplomatic ties. More severe fallout could follow — possibly even war.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/05/opinion/05jonesWeb/05jonesWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/05/opinion/05jonesWeb/05jonesWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/05/opinion/05jonesWeb/05jonesWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/05/opinion/05jonesWeb/05jonesWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/05/opinion/05jonesWeb/05jonesWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/05/opinion/05jonesWeb/05jonesWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/05/opinion/05jonesWeb/05jonesWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Saudi Arabia’s Dangerous Sectarian Game', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saudi Arabia’s Dangerous Game', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Toby Craig', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nimr, Nimr al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunni Muslims', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shiite Muslims', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-05T00:31:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toby Craig Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toby', 'middlename': 'Craig', 'lastname': 'Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/05f597d6-934a-5022-9eec-a688c8eaecbc', 'word_count': 851, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05f597d6-934a-5022-9eec-a688c8eaecbc'}, {'abstract': 'Mary Barra is credited with stabilizing G.M. after the company admitted to selling millions of cars with defective ignition switches that were later tied to at least 124 deaths.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/05/business/gm-chief-is-named-chairwoman-affirming-her-leadership.html', 'snippet': 'Mary Barra is credited with stabilizing G.M. after the company admitted to selling millions of cars with defective ignition switches that were later tied to at least 124 deaths.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — In a move that consolidates power at the top of the nation’s largest automaker, General Motors said on Monday that its