e', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dce781ce-8dbd-5048-9394-d7bcfb69e123', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dce781ce-8dbd-5048-9394-d7bcfb69e123'}, {'abstract': 'Politicians twist themselves into knots to say we should or should not send troops to the Middle East — trying to wage war without actually sacrificing bodies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/magazine/the-empty-threat-of-boots-on-the-ground.html', 'snippet': 'Politicians twist themselves into knots to say we should or should not send troops to the Middle East — trying to wage war without actually sacrificing bodies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since the terrorist attacks of Sept. 11, the United States has fought two very long, very costly and, in the end, not very successful wars. Barack Obama was elected president in no small part because he promised to bind up the nation’s wounds and withdraw its troops. Yet the enemy, in the forms of Al Qaeda and the Islamic State, remains and must be confronted, in part, the Obama administration believes, by the American military. The president has struggled to find a language that distinguishes between force, which most Americans want, and war, which they don’t. He and his team have therefore promised, vowed, pledged that, come what may, they will not use ‘‘boots on the ground.’’', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/magazine/10firstwords/10mag-10firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/ma