e', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eeec138b-d563-5926-811d-fd1074a554f8', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eeec138b-d563-5926-811d-fd1074a554f8'}, {'abstract': 'Ken Griffey Jr. and Mike Piazza, who might make up this year’s Hall of Fame class, would have played together in Flushing if the Mets had been able to make a trade work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/06/sports/baseball/hall-of-fame-ken-griffey-jr-mike-piazza-mets.html', 'snippet': 'Ken Griffey Jr. and Mike Piazza, who might make up this year’s Hall of Fame class, would have played together in Flushing if the Mets had been able to make a trade work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was never happening. Not the way it unfolded, anyway. But that did not stop the Mets from pushing aggressively to bring baseball’s most electrifying player to a team on the rise. Imagine Ken Griffey Jr. and Mike Piazza, who might make up this year’s Cooperstown class, sharing the middle of the lineup in Flushing. The Mets sure did.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/06/sports/SUB-06-Y-KEPNER/SUB-06-Y-KEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/06/sports/SUB-06-Y-KEPNER/SUB-06-Y-KEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 854}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/06/sports/SUB-06-Y-KEPNER/SUB-06-Y-KEPNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 720, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_nam