-by-way-of-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'Making pipian — a thick sauce of seeds and chiles — with help from place and memory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pipian is a classic Mexican sauce, thick with seeds and nuts, spicy with chiles, that takes its name from the pepitas, or pumpkin seeds, that are used in its creation. The version I make makes no claims to authenticity, though. The recipe does not derive from the fieldwork of great Mexican cooking masters like Diana Kennedy or Patricia Quintana (who might rail against it), nor from personal experience cooking in central Mexico with the women and men who have been making such sauces since the region’s pre-Hispanic era. (They might shrug.) It is not red or green, as many pipian sauces are, but a delightful, almost pumpkiny orange and brown, with a texture that approximates the look of washed velvet.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/magazine/10eat1/10mag-10eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/magazine/10eat1/10mag-10eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/magazine/10eat1/10mag-10eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/magazine/10eat1/10mag-10eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1535, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/magazine/10eat1/10mag-10eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 