, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38be18dc-ef76-5cd4-b3b4-800910c6f4e8'}, {'abstract': 'This British historian tells the story of ancient Rome with great authority and relish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/08/books/review-dynasty-tom-hollands-chronicle-of-the-first-five-emperors-who-ruled-ancient-rome.html', 'snippet': 'This British historian tells the story of ancient Rome with great authority and relish.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Nothing could be fainter than those torches which allow us, not to pierce the darkness, but to glimpse it,” wrote the philosopher Seneca, shortly before his former student Nero forced him to commit suicide. And ancient history — where the record is fragmented and riddled with unknown unknowns — is a pictorial parade lit more by flickering torches than by a steady, burning light. Still, with his new book, “Dynasty,” the British historian Tom Holland has crafted a history of early Rome that has all the gripping detail and narrative momentum of a novel.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 831, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 831}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 739, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1478, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Dynasty,’ Tom Holland’s Chronicle of the First Five Emperors Who Ruled Ancient Rome', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Tyrants Behind the Glory That Was Rome', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holland, Tom (1968- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dynasty: The Rise and Fall of the House of Caesar (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-07T21:49:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d17671b-20d4-5d1f-acff-d0d43f1fa15e', 'word_count': 889, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d17671b-20d4-5d1f-acff-d0d43f1fa15e'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Lee joined the drummers Chris Corsano and Ches Smith for an evening of improvisation, part of her three-night residency at Jack in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/08/arts/music/review-the-cellist-okkyung-lee-tests-extended-techniques-boundaries.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Lee joined the drummers Chris Corsano and Ches Smith for an evening of improvisation, part of her three-night residency at Jack in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What Okkyung Lee does on the cello is often called “extended technique,” which means producing sounds by unusual means. But she more or less obliterates the need for the term. When she keeps her fingers choked up on the neck, pointing downward near the bridge, or rubs her bow against the strings to make sluicing sounds and hard, guttural rips, this is her standard; this is her usual. It’s part of her language.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08OKKUNGLEE/08OKKUNGLEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08OKKUNGLEE/08OKKUNGLEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08OKKUNGLEE/08OKKUNGLEE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08OKKUNGLEE/08OKKUNGLEE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08OKKUNGLEE/08OKKUNGLEE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08OKKUNGLEE/08OKKUNGLEE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08OKKUNGLEE/08OKKUNGLEE-thumbLarg