c2f6-0ee7-55b0-acc5-3f2d7c91ba19'}, {'abstract': 'This film considers the cruel paradox of living amid life-enhancing technological miracles that don’t fulfill a yearning for a more purposeful existence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/08/movies/review-anesthesia-ponders-the-meaningfulness-of-life.html', 'snippet': 'This film considers the cruel paradox of living amid life-enhancing technological miracles that don’t fulfill a yearning for a more purposeful existence.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Anesthesia,” Tim Blake Nelson’s exquisitely compressed cri de coeur about the meaning of life in a hyper-connected world, begins as a knife-wielding stranger viciously attacks Walter (Sam Waterston), a wise, kindhearted Columbia University philosophy professor at the door of his apartment building. The circular story immediately backtracks to observe Walter, who is on the verge of retirement after 34 years, addressing a class and posing the same unanswerable questions he has been pondering out loud for more than three decades.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08ANESTHESIA2/08ANESTHESIA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08ANESTHESIA2/08ANESTHESIA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08ANESTHESIA2/08ANESTHESIA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08ANESTHESIA2/08ANESTHESIA2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1320, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy