8a81e5b', 'word_count': 827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9907a58c-d70a-5f6b-8d4e-9ffbe8a81e5b'}, {'abstract': '“Lamb,” with Ross Partridge as its director, star and screenwriter, based his film on a novel by Bonnie Nadzam.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/08/movies/review-visiting-a-lolita-land-of-sorts-with-lamb.html', 'snippet': '“Lamb,” with Ross Partridge as its director, star and screenwriter, based his film on a novel by Bonnie Nadzam.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Lamb,” a slow-burning creep-out about a kidnapping, would like you to see it as a requiem for the lost and the loveless. Set in a present that looks like real life but doesn’t feel like it, the story pivots on its title sad sack, David Lamb, a habitual dissembler and possible sociopath mired in personal and professional crisis. Death, marital and work woes, a young mistress — Lamb has it all when one day he sweeps away an 11-year-old girl, taking her for, well, what exactly is the question that reverberates queasily through this movie, its hotel rooms and weepy moments.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08LAMB/08LAMB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08LAMB/08LAMB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 255}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08LAMB/08LAMB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/arts/08LAMB/08LAMB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 1872, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'c