m', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arango', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34269511-36b8-5f39-b1ee-efad4e4d7139', 'word_count': 1023, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34269511-36b8-5f39-b1ee-efad4e4d7139'}, {'abstract': 'Tsalta Baptiste, who lives in Shaoxing, combined his love of acting and hip-hop to create a trailer for an unmade Tupac Shakur biopic, which drew many viewers online.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/movies/a-self-appointed-herald-of-hip-hop-in-china.html', 'snippet': 'Tsalta Baptiste, who lives in Shaoxing, combined his love of acting and hip-hop to create a trailer for an unmade Tupac Shakur biopic, which drew many viewers online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost from the moment he moved from California to Shaoxing in China’s Zhejiang Province in October 2014, Tsalta Baptiste enjoyed a sense of notoriety. That happens when you’re an African-American man suddenly living in a nation with a population of more than 1.3 billion. Mr. Baptiste’s passing resemblance to Kobe Bryant also helped in a country that reveres the Los Angeles Lakers star.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/arts/10BAPTISTE1/10BAPTISTE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 251, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/arts/10BAPTISTE1/10BAPTISTE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 251}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/arts/10BAPTISTE1/10BAPTISTE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image