', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Raqqa Is Being Slaughtered Silently', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Syrian Observatory for Human Rights', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Raqqa (Syria)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T13:42:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Barnard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78c2f9ed-c699-5b37-8373-fc2fb90534e6', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78c2f9ed-c699-5b37-8373-fc2fb90534e6'}, {'abstract': 'In a new film, Ms. Smith plays an ornery homeless woman. “It was a change from all those hats and clothes in ‘Downton,’” she said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/movies/maggie-smith-shifts-from-dowager-to-down-and-out-for-the-lady-in-the-van.html', 'snippet': 'In a new film, Ms. Smith plays an ornery homeless woman. “It was a change from all those hats and clothes in ‘Downton,’” she said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Old friends, Maggie Smith and the playwright Alan Bennett sat on a sofa at a chic hotel, lobbing lines at each other as they recalled how they first met. “Then she vaulted right over the pig,” Mr. Bennett said. Ms. Smith threw herself back on the sofa and laughed uproariously. “It was terrifying,” she said, wiping her eyes. Mr. Bennett looked at her, straight-faced. “For the pig, too, I imagine,” he said.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/arts/10MAGGIESMITH1/10MAGGIESMITH1-articleLarge-v2.j