umbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/world/asia/20160109Kano_ss-slide-U4IO/20160109Kano_ss-slide-U4IO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/world/asia/20160109Kano_ss-slide-U4IO/20160109Kano_ss-slide-U4IO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/08/world/asia/20160109Kano_ss-slide-U4IO/20160109Kano_ss-slide-U4IO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/world/asia/20160109Kano_ss-slide-U4IO/20160109Kano_ss-slide-U4IO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Costume Maker’s Unlikely Star Factory', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mascots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:19:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/b8157ed2-3e60-5450-955a-94d75eae4933', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/b8157ed2-3e60-5450-955a-94d75eae4933'}, {'abstract': 'Stories sketch the life of an émigré dogged by his past. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/the-sleep-of-the-righteous-by-wolfgang-hilbig.html', 'snippet': 'Stories sketch the life of an émigré dogged by his past. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The brutality, betrayals and deadening conformity of East Germany have provided fodder for a long list of celebrated writers. Christa Wolf won acclaim for her nuanced critiques of the now defunct Communist dictatorship, though her reputation took a beating with her admission that she had served as a \xadStasi informant. Heiner Müller’s scathing dramas challenged the authoritarian regime and earned him scorn as a “historical pessimist.” Then there was Wolfgang Hilbig, born in Saxony in 1941, who shoveled coal in a factory boiler room before discovering his true calling. Permitted to resettle in West Germany in 1985, he began attracting attention for his evocations of the East’s apocalyptic postwar landscape and the dislocations of a Cold War-era émigré who felt like an outsider in both societies. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Hammer/0110-BKS-Hammer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 873, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Hammer/0110-BKS-Hammer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 873}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Hammer/0110-BKS-Hammer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 704, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Hammer/0110-BKS-Hammer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1408, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Hammer/0110-BKS-Hammer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Hammer/0110-BKS-Hammer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Hammer/0110-BKS-Hammer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Sleep of the Righteous,’ by Wolfgang Hilbig', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eyes of the Stasi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hammer, Joshua', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hilbig, Wolfgang (1941-2007)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cole, Isabel Fargo (1973- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Sleep of the Righteous (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:19:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/049de380-09ce-5036-84af-671b74b61288', 'word_count': 622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/049de380-09ce-5036-84af-671b74b61288'}, {'abstract': 'A novel sets the Dominique Strauss-Kahn case in London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/paradise-city-by-elizabeth-day.html', 'snippet': 'A novel sets the Dominique Strauss-Kahn case in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Novels in which separate story lines collide often give the impression that even the most disparate people are somehow connected. And when that collision occurs through an act of violence, it’s sometimes assumed that other differences can be ignored — because, after all, what life has been lived without some pain and suffering? At best, such stories come across as saccharine odes to our shared humanity. But in a novel like Elizabeth Day’s tale of contemporary London, “Paradise City,” they can seem to exploit the circumstances of the less fortunate and the marginalized.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Shafaieh/0110-BKS-Shafaieh-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1070, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Shafaieh/0110-BKS-Shafaieh-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1070}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Shafaieh/0110-BKS-Shafaieh-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 575, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Shafaieh/0110-BKS-Shafaieh-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1149, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Shafaieh/0110-BKS-Shafaieh-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Shafaieh/0110-BKS-Shafaieh-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/0110-BKS-Shafaieh/0110-BKS-Shafaieh-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Paradise City,’ by Elizabeth Day', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Posh Protections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Day, Elizabeth (1978- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Paradise City (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:19:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Shafaieh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shafaieh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/59f6eef0-21cf-59f0-ba7b-efdfcb6af318', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59f6eef0-21cf-59f0-ba7b-efdfcb6af318'}, {'abstract': 'Views on abortion were different before Roe v. Wade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/defenders-of-the-unborn-by-daniel-k-williams.html', 'snippet': 'Views on abortion were different before Roe v. Wade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among those of us who wish to protect access to abortion, it’s easy to feel that “right to life” language is a cover for an attack on feminism. It’s a feeling supported by a common story about \xadhistory: The anti-abortion movement began after Roe v. Wade, because conservative evangelicals were threatened by women’s newfound power over their bodies. What else could explain the movement’s swift rise in the decade following the Supreme Court’s decision, if not a widespread reaction against equal rights?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Defenders of the Unborn,’ by Daniel K. Williams', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shifting Allegiances', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dombek, Kristin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Daniel K (1977- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Defenders of the Unborn: The Pro-Life Movement Before Roe v. Wade (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:20:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kristin Dombek', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kristin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dombek', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d74411f0-a89c-57fa-b56a-fe3eead0bfa4', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d74411f0-a89c-57fa-b56a-fe3eead0bfa4'}, {'abstract': 'The controversial critic Dave Hickey claims the label of feminist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/dave-hickeys-25-women-essays-on-their-art.html', 'snippet': 'The controversial critic Dave Hickey claims the label of feminist.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Most of my favorite people are women,” Dave Hickey proclaims in the introduction to “25 Women.” This statement, and indeed the whole book, can be read as a response to the accusations of sexism that have followed Hickey since the culture wars of the ’90s, when he published “The Invisible Dragon,” a series of cantankerous essays attacking the academic art establishment. According to Hickey, beauty, once the handmaiden of art, had been sacrificed on the altar of political correctness by a censorious ring of tenured apparatchiks. Hickey’s antagonists fought back. For the feminist critic Amelia Jones, his “revival of an abstract notion of ‘beauty’\u200a” served to validate “a particular group of critics (almost all white men) as having access to the truth.” For its critics, beauty was hardly innocent or self-evident, but hampered with cultural baggage: the objectification of women, the exclusion of minorities and the meretricious seductions of the marketplace. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dave Hickey’s ‘25 Women: Essays on Their Art’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who’s a Feminist?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hickey, Dave', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': '25 Women: Essays on Their Art (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:20:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chloe Wyma', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chloe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyma', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/84ffff3b-a011-5b33-9abb-437f7da940a3', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84ffff3b-a011-5b33-9abb-437f7da940a3'}, {'abstract': 'Updated guidelines recommend keeping alcohol intake to less than the equivalent of 