snippet': 'Alex Haley brought attention to black America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1977 Alex Haley, the author of the best-selling “Autobiography of Malcolm X,” achieved world renown after ABC broadcast an eight-part version of his book “Roots,” published the previous year to great acclaim. “Roots,” which chronicled Haley’s search for his African ancestors (and in doing so created a template for the genealogical boom reflected in popular series like “Finding Your Roots,” with Henry Louis Gates Jr.), became an international phenomenon that turned the financially insecure Haley into an icon. Yet Haley’s newfound celebrity and wealth proved to be a double-edged sword. Allegations of plagiarism and historical inaccuracies scarred him, diminishing his literary reputation, and, argues the historian Robert J. Norrell in his briskly paced “Alex Haley: And the Books That Changed a Nation,” unfairly tarnished the author of “the two most influential books on African-American history in the second half of the 20th century.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10subJOSEPH/10subJOSEPH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 882, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10subJOSEPH/10subJOSEPH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 882}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10subJOSEPH/10subJOSEPH-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10subJOSEPH/10subJOSEPH-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1371, 'width': 933, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10subJOSEPH/10subJOSEPH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10subJOSEPH/10subJOSEPH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10subJOSEPH/10subJOSEPH-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Alex Haley: And the Books That Changed a Nation,’ by Robert J. Norrell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deeply Rooted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norrell, Robert J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haley, Alex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Alex Haley: And the Books That Changed a Nation (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:25:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peniel E. Joseph', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Joseph', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/148dfe61-ec93-5254-8c05-9f9f7c8ea0d4', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/148dfe61-ec93-5254-8c05-9f9f7c8ea0d4'}, {'abstract': 'How the repercussions of China’s recently repealed one-child policy have reshaped its society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/one-child-by-mei-fong.html', 'snippet': 'How the repercussions of China’s recently repealed one-child policy have reshaped its society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers would be forgiven for thinking that the announcement, on Oct. 29, 2015, that China was changing its one-child policy would have turned this book from an account of the daily lives of Chinese people into a work of history. Not so. The event itself came rather late for Mei Fong’s “One Child.” But she makes disconcertingly clear that the repercussions of population control will continue to reverberate throughout China. The policy itself remains a monument to official callousness, and Fong’s book pays moving testimony to the suffering and forbearance of its victims.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10PARKER/10PARKER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10PARKER/10PARKER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10PARKER/10PARKER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10PARKER/10PARKER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 858, 'width': 1283, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10PARKER/10PARKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10PARKER/10PARKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10PARKER/10PARKER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘One Child,’ by Mei Fong', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Little Emperors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fong, Mei (1972- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "One Child: The Story of China's Most Radical Experiment (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:26:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/026c5e04-8710-5071-a6db-ac73ff0e9852', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/026c5e04-8710-5071-a6db-ac73ff0e9852'}, {'abstract': 'The short life of Aaron Swartz, a passionate advocate of free information.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/the-idealist-aaron-swartz-and-the-rise-of-free-culture-on-the-internet-by-justin-peters.html', 'snippet': 'The short life of Aaron Swartz, a passionate advocate of free information.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a late autumn day in 2010, Aaron Swartz, an open-information advocate and early partner at the website Reddit, sneaked into a basement closet at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology and surreptitiously connected his laptop to the school’s high-speed Internet network. Using the school’s credentials, he gained access to JSTOR, a database of academic journals, and began to illegally download thousands of files. Once a promising entrepreneur, Swartz had eschewed the traditional Silicon Valley career path for an unorthodox campaign of radical public-interest activism. He believed that the five million documents behind the JSTOR paywall were a common good and that they needed to be liberated by guerrilla action.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10WITT/10WITT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10WITT/10WITT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10WITT/10WITT-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10WITT/10WITT-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1792, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10WITT/10WITT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10WITT/10WITT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10WITT/10WITT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Idealist: Aaron Swartz and the Rise of Free Culture on the Internet,’ by Justin Peters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Portrait of an Activist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peters, Justin (1981- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swartz, Aaron (1986-2013)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Idealist: Aaron Swartz and the Rise of Free Culture on the Internet (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:27:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Witt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d40bf8f7-8ce9-59b5-a1ce-71daa4205f57', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d40bf8f7-8ce9-59b5-a1ce-71daa4205f57'}, {'abstract': 'An author changes genre to take in the world around him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/shame-and-wonder-by-david-searcy.html', 'snippet': 'An author changes genre to take in the world around him.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When people talk about style they are always a little astonished at the newness of it,” F. Scott Fitzgerald once wrote, “because they think that it is only style that they are talking about.” But uncommon literary style is always integrative, both the mother and the daughter of invention, wrought from a writer’s desperation “to express a new idea with such force that it will have the originality of the thought.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10ORANGE/10ORANGE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10ORANGE/10ORANGE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10ORANGE/10ORANGE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10ORANGE/10ORANGE-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1683, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10ORANGE/10ORANGE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10ORANGE/10ORANGE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/books/review/10ORANGE/10ORANGE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Shame and Wonder,’ by David Searcy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sublime Connections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Searcy, David E (1946- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Shame and Wonder: Essays (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:29:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Orange', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orange', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c96bdf0-6ab8-56e0-8880-eb1e550f23e6', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c96bdf0-6ab8-56e0-8880-eb1e550f23e6'}, {'abstract': 'A tour of some of the world’s exceptional places.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/the-geography-of-genius-by-eric-weiner.html', 'snippet': 'A tour of some of the world’s exceptional places.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early 1960s, the Soviet Union tried to make a version of Silicon Valley from scratch. A city called Zelenograd came to life on the outskirts of Moscow and was populated with all manner of brainy Soviet engineers. The hope — naturally — was that a concentration of clever minds coupled with ample funding would result in a wellspring of innovation and help Russia keep pace with California’s electronics boom. The experiment worked as well as one might expect. Few people will read this on a Mayakovsky-branded tablet or \xadsmartphone.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Geography of Genius,’ by Eric Weiner', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Smart Sites', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Eric', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Geography of Genius: A Search for the World's Most Creative Places From Ancient Athens to Silicon Valley (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:29:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashlee Vance', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashlee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vance', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6995b0f2-338a-5c81-8029-9823035a0e33', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6995b0f2-338a-5c81-8029-9823035a0e33'}, {'abstract': 'The final volume of Harris’s Ancient Rome trilogy follows Cicero’s last years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/books/review/dictator-by-robert-harris.html', 'snippet': 'The final volume of Harris’s Ancient Rome trilogy follows Cicero’s last years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cicero, the Roman statesman whose talent for oratory was such that he remains to this day a byword for eloquence, has always divided opinion. A key player in the death agony of Rome’s traditional republican system of government, he was lauded by his admirers as a defender of constitutional propriety and dismissed by his foes as a vacillating opportunist. Posterity has proved similarly conflicted. While America’s founding fathers revered him as a model of civic duty, he was excoriated by the most formidable German classicist of the 19th century, Theodor Mommsen, as a precursor of that lowest class of writer, a “newspaper columnist.” A person’s attitude to Cicero can often be most revealing. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'imag