e': 'subject', 'value': 'Family Leaves', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-08T16:47:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38fca462-d788-51a1-a3a4-8c295e0cb965', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38fca462-d788-51a1-a3a4-8c295e0cb965'}, {'abstract': 'A new settlement should help curb unjustified surveillance of people targeted for personal, political, religious or racial reasons.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/08/a-welcome-move-on-police-surveillance/', 'snippet': 'A new settlement should help curb unjustified surveillance of people targeted for personal, political, religious or racial reasons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than 30 years have passed since the federal courts reacted to the New York City Police Department’s disregard for the Constitution by limiting how far the department could go in conducting surveillance on constitutionally protected activity. The court-ordered guidelines were loosened after 9/11 to give the police more flexibility. But over time, the tendencies that had forced the courts to act in the first place began to reassert themselves. In 2013, for example, the Associated Press showed that the department was using the “terrorism” designation to spy on law-abiding Muslims — and to ignore court-ordered limitations on investigations of constitutionally protected activity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/08/opinion/7staplesWEB/7staplesWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 