-b589-c69b85e87ed7', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f97a1583-06e4-5b6d-b589-c69b85e87ed7'}, {'abstract': 'The Ghetto Film School collaborates with the Frick Collection in a program that exposes students to artwork at the museum and lets them shoot there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/arts/design/young-moviemakers-meet-old-masters-at-the-frick.html', 'snippet': 'The Ghetto Film School collaborates with the Frick Collection in a program that exposes students to artwork at the museum and lets them shoot there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Gilded Age mansion of the Frick Collection, in a room devoted to Jean-Honoré Fragonard’s bucolic wall panels called “The Progress of Love,” two young men meet and begin a flirtatious debate about the four stages of love illustrated in the 18th-century canvases, from a painted couple’s first pastoral encounter to their joyful reminiscing over old love letters. The more worldly of the two men suggests that actual love can be far more treacherous. Indeed, the arc of their relationship eventually bears this out: Far from Fragonard’s happily-ever-after scenario, it ends with a breakup devastating to one of the men. Years later, he advises his teenage niece that pain is a privilege of being human. “Life’s little pleasures are worth rotting for,” he says.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/arts/10FRICK1/10FRICK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/arts/10FRICK1/10FRICK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/arts/10FRICK1/10FRICK1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy':