rant)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-09T19:46:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Starkey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Starkey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0390a565-77b0-51f5-a52f-0fb88c10f449', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0390a565-77b0-51f5-a52f-0fb88c10f449'}, {'abstract': 'Despite a global trend against capital punishment, the U.S. remains among the top five countries that resort to using the death penalty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/opinion/america-and-its-fellow-executioners.html', 'snippet': 'Despite a global trend against capital punishment, the U.S. remains among the top five countries that resort to using the death penalty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aside from the barbarism and injustice of Saudi Arabia’s execution of 47 men on Jan. 2, the state-sponsored killings — some by beheading — bucked a strong trend against capital punishment in most of the world. According to the annual report of Amnesty International, executions were carried out in 22 countries in 2014, the year covered; there were 25 in 2004. The total number of people known to have been executed also fell. To its disgrace, the United States was still among the five countries that most often used capital punishment — alongside China, Saudi Arabia, Iran and Iraq — but the number of executions in America continued to decline.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/10sun3web/10sun3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/10sun3web/10sun3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/10sun3web/10sun3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/10sun3web/10sun3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1303, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/10sun3web/10sun3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/10sun3web/10sun3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/10sun3web/10sun3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'America and Its Fellow Executioners', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'America and Its Fellow Executioners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMNESTY INTERNATIONAL', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Death Penalty Information Center', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amnesties, Commutations and Pardons', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-09T19:50:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/58853cc3-422d-5a1f-9af6-bc85c7c2f826', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58853cc3-422d-5a1f-9af6-bc85c7c2f826'}, {'abstract': 'Newark, N.J., had Cory Booker and Chris Christie. Union City had teachers and parents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/10/opinion/sunday/how-to-fix-the-countrys-failing-schools-and-how-not-to.html', 'snippet': 'Newark, N.J., had Cory Booker and Chris Christie. Union City had teachers and parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A QUARTER-CENTURY ago, Newark and nearby Union City epitomized the failure of American urban school systems. Students, mostly poor minority and immigrant children, were performing abysmally. Graduation rates were low. Plagued by corruption and cronyism, both districts had a revolving door of superintendents. New Jersey officials threatened to take over Union City’s schools in 1989 but gave them a one-year reprieve instead. Six years later, state education officials, decrying the gross mismanagement of the Newark schools, seized control there.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/sunday/10kirpSUB/10kirpSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/10/opinion/sunday/10kirpSUB/10kirpSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':