day from 9 a.m. to 2 p.m. Eastern.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/11/whats-going-on-in-this-picture-jan-11-2016/', 'snippet': 'On Mondays, we publish a Times photo without a caption, headline or other information about its origins. Join the conversation by commenting on what you see and why. A live discussion is offered that day from 9 a.m. to 2 p.m. Eastern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated: Jan. 15, 2015', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Going On in This Picture? | Jan. 11, 2016', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-01-11T10:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/366ad5e9-03c6-574c-903b-9b9868ffed70', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/366ad5e9-03c6-574c-903b-9b9868ffed70'}, {'abstract': 'Living abroad, a novel’s American women face new expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/the-expatriates-by-janice-y-k-lee.html', 'snippet': 'Living abroad, a novel’s American women face new expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A female, funny Henry James in Asia, Janice Y. K. Lee is vividly good on the subject of Americans abroad. “The Expatriates,” her second novel, is set like her first, “The Piano Teacher,” in Hong Kong, and the two books taken together are a rich education in an almost century of cruelty, exploitation, deep pockets and good parties in the city — a setting that becomes a complicated character in its own right. In her new book, Lee observes an affluent community of contemporary Westerners, but more specifically, those who “crossed over into that other country of motherhood,” another foreign land requiring expatriation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17POUNCY/17POUNCY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 897, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17POUNCY/17POUNCY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 897}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17POUNCY/17POUNCY-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17POUNCY/17POUNCY-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1533, 'width': 1025, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17POUNCY/17POUNCY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17POUNCY/17POUNCY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17POUNCY/17POUNCY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Expatriates,’ by Janice Y. K. Lee', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lost in Hong Kong', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Janice Y K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Expatriates (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-11T10:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maggie Pouncey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pouncey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7c63204-7211-5183-bd2e-1303e58032d3', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7c63204-7211-5183-bd2e-1303e58032d3'}, {'abstract': 'The global trade in citizenship serves many functions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/the-cosmopolites-the-coming-of-the-global-citizen-by-atossa-araxia-abrahamian.html', 'snippet': 'The global trade in citizenship serves many functions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2008 the United Arab Emirates came up with a novel solution to their bidoon “problem” — the presence of thousands of stateless persons within their territories. Most bidoon descend from families who resided there long before the creation of the U.A.E.; many were from nomadic tribes and simply failed to register as nationals at the time. Yet they have been denied citizenship. That status would give them access to the myriad benefits accorded full members of this wealthy oil state — from free health care and education, to a guaranteed income, subsidized utilities, a marriage bonus, land and an interest-free house loan. Instead, they are categorized as illegal immigrants. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17BELLAMY/17BELLAMY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17BELLAMY/17BELLAMY-arti