], 'headline': {'main': 'This Land Is Our Land', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'This Land Is Our Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Lands', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oregon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Malheur National Wildlife Refuge (Oregon)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-11T21:34:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/47219313-af71-56b8-aadd-f6b3950b2f41', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47219313-af71-56b8-aadd-f6b3950b2f41'}, {'abstract': 'A Times article on April 15, 1858, out of Havana mentioned the technology, but it would be two decades before Thomas Edison’s name appeared.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/12/science/the-arrival-of-electric-light.html', 'snippet': 'A Times article on April 15, 1858, out of Havana mentioned the technology, but it would be two decades before Thomas Edison’s name appeared.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since the early 19th century, inventors had been tinkering with various methods of using electricity to produce light. The New York Times first wrote of the technology on April 15, 1858. That day, “Our Own Correspondent” in Havana described celebrations of Holy Week that included “an electric light” cast across the harbor “revealing the name of Queen Isabel.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Arrival of Electric Light', 'kicker': 'First Mention', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Describing Electric Light, Even Before Edison', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bakalar, Nicholas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light Bulbs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edison, Thomas A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-11T21:35:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7d15deb-df33-597d-a7a2-13ab7117b72b', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7d15deb-df33-597d-a7a2-13ab7117b72b'}, {'abstract': 'Governor Christie, who first won office saying he would toughen laws regulating firearms, has used his recent record in New Jersey to recast himself as a champion of the Second Amendment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/12/nyregion/chris-christie-makes-steady-rightward-shift-on-guns.html', 'snippet': 'Governor Christie, who first won office saying he would toughen laws regulating firearms, has used his recent record in New Jersey to recast himself as a champion of the Second Amendment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Democratic attack ad from 2009 showed Chris Christie’s face beside a row of bullets, branding him as an ally of the National Rifle Association. A narrator warned that unlike Jon S. Corzine, a Democrat who was then New Jersey’s governor, Mr. Christie “stands with the N.R.A. and opposes banning armor-piercing rifles.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/12/nyregion/12CHRISTIE/12CHRISTIE-articleLarg