, '_id': 'nyt://video/ebbf6add-3c22-53d9-8977-f8e80cc29f71', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/ebbf6add-3c22-53d9-8977-f8e80cc29f71'}, {'abstract': 'Also, pick your pasta and sauce at Aunt Jake’s; high-end food stalls near Pennsylvania Station; and movies and meatballs in Bushwick.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/13/dining/momofuku-nishi-david-chang-chelsea.html', 'snippet': 'Also, pick your pasta and sauce at Aunt Jake’s; high-end food stalls near Pennsylvania Station; and movies and meatballs in Bushwick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Momofuku Nishi Word got out last Friday that David Chang would open his latest venture that evening at 6. A long line had formed outside the Chelsea restaurant by 5 p.m. By 6:15, plates of food were streaming to the bare wood communal tables. Mr. Chang was there, baseball cap pulled low, describing how he had originally envisioned a fast-food model but changed his mind in favor of a restaurant with a decidedly eclectic menu. It hints of Italy, but in the hands of Joshua Pinsky, the chef, the bagna cauda winks at Caesar salad, and cacio e pepe swaps out the cheese for fermented chickpeas to become ceci e pepe. There’s no shortage of noodles, and the menu is deciphered with playful footnotes. Reservations will be taken in a few weeks: 232 Eighth Avenue (West 22nd Street), 646-518-1919, nishi.momofuku.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/13/dining/13OFF/13OFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/13/dining/13OFF/13OFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/13/dining/13OFF/13OFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_na