e.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should I Tell Mom I Think Dad Was Gay?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Should I Tell Mom I Think Dad Was Gay?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicists, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appiah, Kwame Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lying', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-13T12:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ad28bc7-01bf-5c99-af93-c05145686c2b', 'word_count': 1440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ad28bc7-01bf-5c99-af93-c05145686c2b'}, {'abstract': 'The poet talks about running for president, voting as a sexual act and gender vacationing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/magazine/eileen-myles-wants-men-to-take-a-hike.html', 'snippet': 'The poet talks about running for president, voting as a sexual act and gender vacationing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many people would be surprised to hear that according to you, poetry is alive and well in America. Our national political conversation has recently seen some rather unpoetic lurches to the right. How do you make sense of that? Poetry always, always, always is a key piece of democracy. It’s like the un-Trump: The poet is the charismatic loser. You’re the fool in Shakespeare; you’re the loose cannon. As things get worse, poetry gets better, because it becomes more necessary.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/magazine/17talk/17mag-17talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1295, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/magazine/17talk/17mag-17talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1295}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/magazine/17talk/17mag-17talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 475, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/magazine/17talk/17mag-17talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 695, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/magazine/17talk/17mag-17talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/17/magazine/17talk/17mag-17talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/magazine/17talk/17mag-17talk-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eileen Myles Wants Men to Take a Hike', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eileen Myles Wants Men to Take a Hike', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cox, Ana Marie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Myles, Eileen (1949- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-13T12:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ana Marie Cox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ana', 'middlename': 'Marie', 'lastname': 'Cox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4521e407-5f6f-5adc-8d2d-8884fd23cc4f', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4521e407-5f6f-5adc-8d2d-8884fd23cc4f'}, {'abstract': 'In a new ad on Tuesday night, Mrs. Clinton did not name Mr. Sanders, but she didn’t have to. Mrs. Clinton, who was such a prohibitive leader in polls that her presence in the race discouraged some Democratic elected officials from challenging her, finds herself in a tightening contest with Mr. Sanders, a self-described Democratic socialist and independent from Vermont.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2016/01/13/as-bernie-sanders-gains-momentum-hillary-clinton-shows-signs-of-noticing/', 'snippet': 'In a new ad on Tuesday night, Mrs. Clinton did not name Mr. Sanders, but she didn’t have to. Mrs. Clinton, who was such a prohibitive leader in polls that her presence in the race discouraged some Democratic elected officials from challenging her,...', 'lead_paragraph': 'When President Obama addressed Congress \u202aon Tuesday night, Hillary Clinton’s campaign had an ad ready for the occasion. The digital spot, which also aired on cable television, showed Mrs. Clinton speaking to the camera and invoking Mr. Obama’s push for new gun control measures as a chance for voters to decide whether candidates stand with the president or against him. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/12/us/12firstdraftnl-clinton/12firstdraftnl-clinton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/12/us/12firstdraftnl-clinton/12firstdraftnl-clinton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/12/us/12firstdraftnl-clinton/12firstdraftnl-clinton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/12/us/12firstdraftnl-clinton/12firstdraftnl-clinton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/12/us/12firstdraftnl-clinton/12firstdraftnl-clinton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/12/us/12firstdraftnl-clinton/12firstdraftnl-clinton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbna