opy was appropriately enticing (but when is it ever not?), and it opened by categorizing the work as “literary suspense.” Immediately the pedant in me reared its caviling head: Aren’t all novels literary, in that they’re assembled from letters and words? (From the Latin litteratura, writing composed of letters of the alphabet.) And aren’t all novels to some extent suspenseful — or else why would we ever finish one? Surely there must always be a managed momentum toward finding out what happens in the end, however slight that may be?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Child/0117-BKS-Child-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Child/0117-BKS-Child-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 695}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Child/0117-BKS-Child-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 885, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Child/0117-BKS-Child-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1631, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Child/0117-BKS-Child-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Child/0117-BKS-Child-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Child/0117-BKS-Child-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Hunters in the Dark,’ by Lawrence Osborne', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'He’s Not Coming Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Child, Lee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Osborne, Lawrence', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Hunters in the Dark (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-13T15:31:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lee Child', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Child', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1303b7ea-06f5-5db7-9b27-4cfe538c394e', 'word_count': 1190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1303b7ea-06f5-5db7-9b27-4cfe538c394e'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist becomes part of the story of an outlawed marriage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/the-lovers-afghanistans-romeo-and-juliet-byrod-nordland.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist becomes part of the story of an outlawed marriage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sentimental title of “The Lovers” suggests a hopeful tale of youthful romance, of passion and perseverance against the backdrop of a war-ravaged Afghanistan. Zakia and Ali, the journalist Rod Nordland’s Afghan Romeo and Juliet, are Tajik and Hazara, Sunni and Shia, disparate ethnicities and rived sects. They live in Bamiyan, where the Taliban destroyed two famed sandstone Buddhas in 2001. They fall in love as teenagers, exchanging flirty glances in the fields of their village, skirting elders and convention. Soon their parents find out; marriage is deemed impossible, and Zakia runs away to a shelter. The two elope but remain sentenced to a life on the run, with Ali facing criminal charges after Zakia’s family lodges a kidnapping case against him.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ZAKARIA/17Zakaria-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ZAKARIA/17Zakaria-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ZAKARIA/17Zakaria-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ZAKARIA/17Zakaria-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ZAKARIA/17ZAKARIA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ZAKARIA/17ZAKARIA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ZAKARIA/17ZAKARIA-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main':