me': 'Moore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecfab3c7-92f2-5d99-b294-b525145aa8ce', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecfab3c7-92f2-5d99-b294-b525145aa8ce'}, {'abstract': 'More than 4,600 educators have signed an open letter speaking out against intolerance and discrimination against Arab and Muslim Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/13/educators-against-intolerance/', 'snippet': 'More than 4,600 educators have signed an open letter speaking out against intolerance and discrimination against Arab and Muslim Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than 4,600 educators in the United States have signed an open letter speaking out against intolerance and discrimination, particularly towards Arab and Muslim Americans. They represent more than 500 academic institutions spanning at least 60 different disciplines, and include 6 Nobel laureates and current and past university presidents.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Educators Against Intolerance', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-13T17:29:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3a753c3-a8e0-586a-86a4-442c4f52c61d', 'word_count': 512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3a753c3-a8e0-586a-86a4-442c4f52c61d'}, {'abstract': 'Under a proposed law, asylum seekers who arrive with more than about $1,450 in cash would have to give up belongings like gold and jewelry to finance their stay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/14/world/europe/denmark-refugees-confiscate-valuables.html', 'snippet': 'Under a proposed law, asylum seekers who arrive with more than about $1,450 in cash would have to give up belongings like gold and jewelry to finance their stay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Denmark is poised to pass a law requiring newly arrived refugees to hand over valuables, including gold or jewelry, to help pay for the costs of lodging them. Under the proposal, asylum seekers who enter the country with more than 10,000 kroner, or about $1,450, in assets would have to help finance their stay.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Denmark Moves to Make Refugees Hand Over Valuables', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Denmark Moves to Seize Valuables From Refugees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and Africa Migrant Crisis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Denmark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rasmussen, Lars Lokke', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-13T17:41:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Bilefsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilefsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25fecc2e-8767-50ec-80ec-6c7af9719f30', 'word_count': 817, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25fecc2e-8767-50ec-80ec-6c7af9719f30'}, {'abstract': 'The move from Connecticut is an effort to tap the high-tech environs, with its cluster of research universities, for knowledge and recruiting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/14/technology/ge-boston-headquarters.html', 'snippet': 'The move from Connecticut is an effort to tap the high-tech environs, with its cluster of research universities, for knowledge and recruiting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'General Electric, the nation’s largest industrial company, makes products like jet engines, locomotives and gas turbines. Its corporate reputation is tied to producing big, heavy equipment — atoms by the ton, not the ephemeral bits of software.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/14/business/14electric-web1/14electric-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/14/business/14electric-web1/14electric-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/14/business/14electric-web1/14electric-web1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/14/business/14electric-web1/14electric-web1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1227, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/14/business/14electric-web1/14electric-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/14/business/14electric-web1/14electric-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 't