ename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16d9a8a9-67cd-5f2f-a2d6-eb79dc8ffc66', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16d9a8a9-67cd-5f2f-a2d6-eb79dc8ffc66'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Japan begins to oil propaganda guns in Mexico in 1941.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/14/1941-japan-opens-propaganda-office/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Japan begins to oil propaganda guns in Mexico in 1941.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — Japan began to oil propaganda guns in Mexico today [Jan. 14] for immediate use in a twofold fight against United States economic penetration and against the newly announced plan of the Roosevelt administration to block Axis purchases of Mexico’s war minerals and oil. High authorities disclosed that representatives of Domei, the official Japanese news agency, have established offices in this capital for distribution of news free of charge to Mexican and other Latin-American newspapers. The United States has no propaganda agency to combat this obvious effort by Japan to influence the Americas.\xa0— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Jan. 15, 1941', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1941: Japan Opens Propaganda Office', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Propaganda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico',