'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1f2457a-fa02-525e-9bb2-cb0c5a2f1dbe', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1f2457a-fa02-525e-9bb2-cb0c5a2f1dbe'}, {'abstract': 'Plus: repayment for a funeral visit, standing up for not sitting down, and deciphering backhanded compliments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/fashion/relationship-woes-funerals-backhanded-compliments.html', 'snippet': 'Plus: repayment for a funeral visit, standing up for not sitting down, and deciphering backhanded compliments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My boyfriend and I live together. We split the cost of food and household goods 50-50. Recently, I was found to have an autoimmune problem that requires using more expensive cleaning products. My boyfriend was upset about splitting the cost. Living alone, he said, he would not use them. (He doesn’t complain that I do all the cleaning.) Still, I proposed a 75-25 split, and he accepted. Now I feel bitter. We’re only talking $20 a month, but it feels as if he doesn’t appreciate my labor or sympathize with my condition. But I have to honor the compromise I suggested, right?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url'