, {'abstract': 'NASA announced on Thursday the companies it had selected for a second round of contracts to take cargo to the International Space Station.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/15/science/contracts-announced-for-runs-to-space-station.html', 'snippet': 'NASA announced on Thursday the companies it had selected for a second round of contracts to take cargo to the International Space Station.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASA announced on Thursday the companies it had selected for a second round of contracts to take cargo to the International Space Station. Two of the contracts will go to companies already performing that task: the Space Exploration Technologies Corporation of Hawthorne, Calif., better known as SpaceX, and Orbital ATK of Dulles, Va. NASA also selected the Sierra Nevada Corporation of Sparks, Nev., which has been developing a mini space shuttle known as Dream Chaser. The first mission is to fly in 2019, and each company will operate at least six cargo missions. NASA officials said the new contracts would provide many more options than they currently have. The Dream Chaser will be able to land like a plane on a runway, allowing payloads like scientific experiments to be unloaded within a few hours. The three contracts, which run through 2024, when the space station is scheduled to be retired, have a maximum value of $14 billion, but NASA officials said they did not expect to spend nearly that much. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Contracts Announced for Runs to Space Station', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Contracts Announced for Runs to Space Station', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Space Exploration Technologies Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orbital ATK Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sierra Nevada Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Space Station', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space and Astronomy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T01:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8763045-2fac-5521-be4a-9838ffda7dbb', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8763045-2fac-5521-be4a-9838ffda7dbb'}, {'abstract': 'FIFA said that Real Madrid and Atlético Madrid could not sign new players until the summer of 2017 because they had improperly signed players younger than 18.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/15/sports/soccer/real-madrid-says-transfer-ban-over-youth-signings-is-unfair.html', 'snippet': 'FIFA said that Real Madrid and Atlético Madrid could not sign new players until the summer of 2017 because they had improperly signed players younger than 18.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADRID — Real Madrid responded on Thursday to a transfer ban imposed by FIFA, soccer’s global governing body, by claiming the sanction was unfairly linked to some youth players whose relatives were already employees of the club, including two sons of its new coach, Zinedine Zidane.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/15/sports/soccer/15madrid2/15madrid2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'su