zation': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed1d127d-396f-5c90-a07a-524463687d30', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed1d127d-396f-5c90-a07a-524463687d30'}, {'abstract': 'Forget drugs. Forget El Chapo. A new generation of gangsters is trying to take over local governments themselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/opinion/sunday/why-cartels-are-killing-mexicos-mayors.html', 'snippet': 'Forget drugs. Forget El Chapo. A new generation of gangsters is trying to take over local governments themselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mexico City — ON the morning of Jan. 2, a team of hired killers set off for the home of 33-year-old Gisela Mota, who only hours before had been sworn in as the first female mayor of Temixco, a sleepy spa town an hour from Mexico City. Ms. Mota was still in her pajamas as the men approached her parents’ breezeblock house. She was in the bedroom, but most of her family was in the front room, cooing over a newborn baby. As the family prepared a milk bottle, the assassins smashed the door open. Amid the commotion, Ms. Mota came out of her bedroom and said firmly, “I am Gisela.” In front of her terrified family, the men beat Ms. Mota and shot her several times, killing her.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/opinion/sunday/17grillo/17grillo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 761, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/opinion/sunday/17grillo/17grillo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 761}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/opinion/sunday/17grillo/17grillo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 808, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption