s/2016/01/16/nyregion/16VALUES-listy/16VALUES-listy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/nyregion/16VALUES-listy/16VALUES-listy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/nyregion/16VALUES-listy/16VALUES-listy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1239, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/nyregion/16VALUES-listy/16VALUES-listy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/16/nyregion/16VALUES-listy/16VALUES-listy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/nyregion/16VALUES-listy/16VALUES-listy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Yorkers Quickly Unite Against Cruz After ‘New York Values’ Comment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Ted', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:07:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Santora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7176921-f8c1-5e55-8cb6-6ffa2e7c5780', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7176921-f8c1-5e55-8cb6-6ffa2e7c5780'}, {'abstract': 'Why I’m not yet resigned to a Trump vs. Cruz race.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/15/they-have-not-yet-begun-to-fight/', 'snippet': 'Why I’m not yet resigned to a Trump vs. Cruz race.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In last night’s Republican debate we finally got the Donald Trump vs. Ted Cruz clash everyone has been pining for\xa0— not on policy, of course, but on the crucial questions of Cruz’s Canuckishness and Trump’s “New York values.” Cruz came prepared on both exchanges, and like others I would say that he won the night, though he did disappear for longish stretches later on and took some strafing fire from Marco Rubio (very) late in the evening. But then I also agree with the observers\xa0who think Trump fundamentally had a good night as well: The Donald has learned a lot about debating since this show started, and even when placed on the defensive he was effective and disciplined —\xa0responding to Cruz on the Canadian issue with more puckishness than bluster and then pivoting smoothly to 9/11 on the “New York values” issue. And then there was this moment, when a question about Nikki Haley’s post-SOTU critique of the party’s “angriest voices” allowed Trump to deliver\xa0— pretty calmly and evenly\xa0— what was probably the line of the night, if not of his campaign: “She did say there was anger. I’m very angry because our country is being run horribly and I will gladly accept the mantle of anger.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'They Have Not Yet Begun To Fight', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Ted', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haley, Nikki R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubio, Marco', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:07:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41ec6881-0846-5490-a7a2-26d1fb5f484b', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41ec6881-0846-5490-a7a2-26d1fb5f484b'}, {'abstract': 'An intimate look inside the world’s largest refugee settlement, on the Kenya-Somalia border.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/city-of-thorns-by-ben-rawlence.html', 'snippet': 'An intimate look inside the world’s largest refugee settlement, on the Kenya-Somalia border.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2010, Ben Rawlence, then a researcher for Human Rights Watch, visited the refugee camp of Dadaab on the eastern Kenyan border, home to 300,000 people, many of whom had fled the chronic civil war in Somalia. The next year, he returned for what would be the first of seven long visits to follow the lives of nine of its inhabitants and to watch the camp grow until it became the largest refugee settlement in the world, more town than camp, with its own hospitals, cinemas and soccer teams, occupying an area the size of New Orleans. It would be good to be able to say that his story is encouraging or uplifting; but Rawlence’s “City of Thorns” is a deeply disturbing and depressing portrait of the violence, destitution, fear, sense of hopelessness and neglect in which a large number of the world’s estimated 60 million forcibly displaced people now live.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Cover/0117-BKS-Cover-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 940, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Cover/0117-BKS-Cover-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 940}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Cover/0117-BKS-Cover-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 654, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Cover/0117-BKS-Cover-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1246, 'width': 795, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Cover/0117-BKS-Cover-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Cover/0117-BKS-Cover-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/0117-BKS-Cover/0117-BKS-Cover-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘City of Thorns,’ by Ben Rawlence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From One Hell to Another', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moorehead, Caroline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rawlence, Ben', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "City of Thorns: Nine Lives in the World's Largest Refugee Camp (Book)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:08:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Moorehead', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moorehead', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/08534fc8-e04b-5e93-a070-7752cde90942', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08534fc8-e04b-5e93-a070-7752cde90942'}, {'abstract': 'But a Kenyan military spokesman said that the attack had targeted a Somali military camp, next to the African Union base, and that most of the dead were Somalis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/16/world/africa/shabab-african-union-somalia.html', 'snippet': 'But a Kenyan military spokesman said that the attack had targeted a Somali military camp, next to the African Union base, and that most of the dead were Somalis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOGADISHU, Somalia — The Shabab militant group said