tiring-adventure-web1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Goodbye, Golf Clubs. Hello, Hiking Boots and Kayak.', 'kicker': 'Retiring', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Goodbye, Golf Clubs. Hello, Hiking Boots and Kayak.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellin, Abby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Recreational Equipment Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hikes and Hiking', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Adventure Travel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Juvenile Diabetes Research Foundation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volunteers and Community Service', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camps and Camping', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:26:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Ellin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ellin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e927975-a038-545f-a93f-c754b36843b9', 'word_count': 1257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e927975-a038-545f-a93f-c754b36843b9'}, {'abstract': 'Alan Dean Foster’s movie novelization, new at No. 1, is the first “Force Awakens” tie-in to hit the adult hardcover list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Alan Dean Foster’s movie novelization, new at No. 1, is the first “Force Awakens” tie-in to hit the adult hardcover list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The List Awakens: Alan Dean Foster’s movie novelization “Star Wars: The Force Awakens” goes right to the top in its first week on the hardcover fiction list. This isn’t the only best seller to spring from Disney’s reboot — an illustrated junior novelization has spent three weeks on the middle-grade hardcover list, where it’s currently No.\u202f2, and other titles have landed on the kids’ series list and the miscellaneous list — but it is the first “Force Awakens” tie-in to hit adult hardcover. So what, besides Chewbacca’s drunken sex scene, makes this an adult book? (I’m only kidding about Chewie — sorry! — but readers curious about the mating habits of wookies can turn to The Guardian, where an Edinburgh zookeeper reports that “I would assume they would do it pretty much the same way we do it.” If you’re like me, this will have you humming Cole Porter for days.) In fact, Foster’s novelization does contain some scenes of graphic violence missing from the movie, but the most adult thing about it may be the florid vocabulary. Here’s how it describes the villain Kylo Ren, for instance: “saturnine of aspect, lithe of build, tortured of mien and troubled of eye.” Keep the children away.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/15/books/review/15list-force/15list-force-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 465, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/15/books/review/15list-force/15list-force-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 465, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/15/books/review/15list-force/15list-force-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/15/books/review/15list-force/15list-force-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/15/books/review/15list-force/15list-force-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:27:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/879f01c0-8a8b-5505-bb9d-6fa9836e3278', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/879f01c0-8a8b-5505-bb9d-6fa9836e3278'}, {'abstract': 'Many in the Indonesian capital expressed astonishment that the assailants had killed only two people, and praised the police for quickly neutralizing the attackers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/16/world/asia/jakarta-bomb-attacks.html', 'snippet': 'Many in the Indonesian capital expressed astonishment that the assailants had killed only two people, and praised the police for quickly neutralizing the attackers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JAKARTA, Indonesia — If the militants who attacked the center of this city with explosives and guns on Thursday hoped to inspire fear and attract followers, they seem to have failed. Instead, life in Jakarta, the Indonesian capital, returned to normal on Friday, with traffic jams, long lines for public transportation and hasty breakfasts at streetside food stalls.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/world/16indonesia/16indonesia-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/16/world/16indonesia/16indonesia-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/world/16indonesia/16indonesia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/world/16indonesia/16indonesia-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/world/16indonesia/16indonesia-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/16/world/16indonesia/16indonesia-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/16/world/16indonesia/16indonesia-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Many in Jakarta Seem to Shrug Off Terrorist Attack', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jakarta (Indonesia)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indonesia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:29:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Cochrane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cochrane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d71415c-0ed6-5018-a5ea-9628ec6177b5', 'word_count': 993, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d71415c-0ed6-5018-a5ea-9628ec6177b5'}, {'abstract': 'The heroes of the first book of Bracken’s new series come from families that can leap through time and space.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/passenger-by-alexandra-bracken.html', 'snippet': 'The heroes of the first book of Bracken’s new series come from families that can leap through time and space.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In present-day New York City, a focused young woman prepares for a recital on the violin, the instrument for which she has sacrificed both her social life and a sense of closeness to her peripatetic mother. She feels the pressure of making good on her training, along with confusion about her best path to the future she desires. While not every teenager is a violin prodigy, Etta’s situation shares common themes with the lives of many driven young people, and right from the opening moments of “Passenger” there is much to like about her. When Etta’s elderly teacher, the most supportive figure in her life, becomes suddenly discouraging on the night of the recital, and Etta overhears an argument between her teacher and her mother as she is about to go onstage, the dramatic tension is ratcheted up, promising an engaging narrative about ambition and family. But this is an adventure story, the first in a new series from Alexandra Bracken, the author of the Darkest Minds books, and Bracken is only just getting started.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Passenger,’ by Alexandra Bracken', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tunnels in Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bracken, Alexandra (1987- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Passenger (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:29:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Jobin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jobin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/64985def-34c4-5f95-82e2-ea4cb276900a', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64985def-34c4-5f95-82e2-ea4cb276900a'}, {'abstract': 'A middle-grade novel recounts a Japanese-American family’s life in a World War II detention camp.  ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/paper-wishes-by-lois-sepahban.html', 'snippet': 'A middle-grade novel recounts a Japanese-American family’s life in a World War II detention camp.  ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The psychic pain from the forced removal and detention of American citizens and residents of Japanese ancestry during World War II is still felt profoundly today. Case in point: a nationwide protest last April that blocked a scheduled auction of artifacts made in United States detention centers, led by descendants of the internees, who could not tolerate the idea that remnants of this dark chapter of their families’ history were up for sale to the highest bidder.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Paper Wishes,’ by Lois Sepahban', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Girl of Internment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sepahban, Lois M (1974- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Paper Wishes (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:30:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Naomi Hirahara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Naomi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirahara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/99e5393e-0ca8-530d-aff3-10e3ba75f90b', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99e5393e-0ca8-530d-aff3-10e3ba75f90b'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Ted Cruz of Texas described Donald J. Trump as having “New York values” in the Republican debate on Thursday. What does that mean exactly?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2016/01/16/nyregion/new-york-values-trump-cruz.html', 'snippet': 'Senator Ted Cruz of Texas described Donald J. Trump as having “New York values” in the Republican debate on Thursday. What does that mean exactly?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Senator Ted Cruz of Texas described Donald J. Trump as having “New York values” in the Republican debate on Thursday. What does that mean exactly?', '