117-BKS-Ali-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1507, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ALI/17ALI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ALI/17ALI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ALI/17ALI-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Illegal,’ by Lawrence Hill', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When I Came I Was a Stranger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, Lawrence (1957- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Illegal (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:36:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Ali', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ali', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d8f6fc2-13e6-5c46-904d-2c2fc75d1b58', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d8f6fc2-13e6-5c46-904d-2c2fc75d1b58'}, {'abstract': 'Fiction inspired by the 1999 World Trade Organization protests. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/your-heart-is-a-muscle-the-size-of-a-fist-by-sunil-yapa.html', 'snippet': 'Fiction inspired by the 1999 World Trade Organization protests. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the dwindling of the anti-\xadglobalization movement over the past decade, the 1999 World Trade Organization protests in Seattle can now look like easily mocked millennial optimism: turtles, teamsters, Wiccans, the “Free Mumia” crowd and beanie-wearing save-the-\xadforests types finding common cause with the victims of corporate capital and glowing with the belief in a better world. Although media coverage of what transpired over the protests’ five chaotic days in November focused on police brutality and destruction of property, in its full reality, the “Battle in Seattle” swung from hope-filled carnival to angry riot to a moment of real change — a polyvalent, ambiguous and high-stakes drama. In other words, a novelist’s dream.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17HENDRIX/0117-BKS-Hendrix-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17HENDRIX/0117-BKS-Hendrix-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 697}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17HENDRIX/0117-BKS-Hendrix-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17HENDRIX/0117-BKS-Hendrix-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17HENDRIX/0117-BKS-Hendrix-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17HENDRIX/0117-BKS-Hendrix-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17HENDRIX/0117-BKS-Hendrix-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Your Heart Is a Muscle the Size of a Fist,’ by Sunil Yapa', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Showdown in Seattle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yapa, Sunil S (1977- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Your Heart Is a Muscle the Size of a Fist (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:36:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Hendrix', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hendrix', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/10f7d9a0-1845-5792-afe1-64e9fbce83d8', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10f7d9a0-1845-5792-afe1-64e9fbce83d8'}, {'abstract': 'Betancourt’s heroine sees things through others’ eyes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/the-blue-line-by-ingrid-betancourt.html', 'snippet': 'Betancourt’s heroine sees things through others’ eyes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The literary style known as magic realism, incorporating fantastic elements into otherwise realistic fiction, has been deployed with much success by Latin American writers. It is also, lately, a Colombian tourism slogan. Now the Colombian politician Ingrid Betancourt, best known for a memoir describing her six years in the jungle as the hostage of \xadanti-government rebels, has added her first novel to the ranks of the genre.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Blue Line,’ by Ingrid Betancourt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Among the Disappeared', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burleigh, Nina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Betancourt, Ingrid', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Blue Line (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:37:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nina Burleigh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burleigh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/724d3072-7621-5f7d-998b-c53acd988650', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/724d3072-7621-5f7d-998b-c53acd988650'}, {'abstract': 'A story of Iranians who studied in England in the 19th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/the-love-of-strangers-by-nile-green.html', 'snippet': 'A story of Iranians who studied in England in the 19th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Great was the weight placed on young shoulders in the spring of 1815 when a small group of students left their home city of Tabriz in northwest Iran and set out for “Inglistan,” the land of the English. Led by the charismatic and doggedly determined Mirza Salih, they were taliban in the truest sense of the word — seekers of knowledge. Their desire for ulum-i-\xadjadid, or new sciences, had become a matter of national security. Wars with Russia had battered the domain of the crown prince, Abbas Mirza, for more than a century, and he was eager to protect it from further destruction. When the young men were sent to the West, their instructions were clear, if daunting: They were to learn all they could in order to drag their country into an industrialized, czar-beating era.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ANAND/17ANAND-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ANAND/17ANAND-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 669}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ANAND/17ANAND-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 918, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ANAND/17ANAND-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1446, 'width': 1296, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ANAND/17ANAND-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ANAND/17ANAND-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/17/books/review/17ANAND/17ANAND-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Love of Strangers,’ by Nile Green', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Inglistan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, Nile', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Love of Strangers: What Six Muslim Students Learned in Jane Austen's London (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-15T16:38:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Anand', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anand', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d949d830-516b-5ece-b321-ab7757a3bce1', 'word_count': 632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d949d830-516b-5ece-b321-ab7757a3bce1'}, {'abstract': 'A novel pairs a Muslim’s arrest in present-day Scotland with a captivity narrative set in the Caucasian War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/17/books/review/the-kindness-of-enemies-by-leila-aboulela.html', 'snippet': 'A novel pairs a Muslim’s arrest in present-day Scotland with a captivity narrative set in the Caucasian War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps we only imagine that questions of allegiance, of belonging, are more urgent now than they ever were. The “war on terror” asks for a pledge of loyalty: Whose side are you on? The question seems straightforward, but as Leila Aboulela’s new novel reminds us, to ask where we come from, to choose where we belong, is not a simple matter at all.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima