i and Rod Nordland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Waqar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rod', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nordland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76db6699-ea7b-5064-81bf-3fc6a09d5aeb', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76db6699-ea7b-5064-81bf-3fc6a09d5aeb'}, {'abstract': 'A Russian doctor’s mission to deliver a much-needed vaccine in a snowstorm is plagued by dangers and temptations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/19/books/review-in-vladimir-sorokins-the-blizzard-a-doctors-long-and-strange-trip.html', 'snippet': 'A Russian doctor’s mission to deliver a much-needed vaccine in a snowstorm is plagued by dangers and temptations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With his latest novel, “The Blizzard,” Vladimir Sorokin has written a small, strange story that has little to do with his current reputation as a Russian literary bad boy and everything to do with riffing on Russia’s fabled literary past. “The Blizzard” begins as a kind of Chekhovian tale about a doctor setting off in a snowstorm on a medical mission to a village. It soon evolves into a phantasmagorical allegory sprinkled with surrealistic scenes that recall Gogol’s “The Nose” and Bulgakov’s “The Master and Margarita.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/19/arts/19BOOKSOROKIN/19BOOKSOROKIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/19/arts/19BOOKSOROKIN/19BOOKSOROKIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'