into contact with three German raiders, including the 10,000-ton Narvik, which they said was known as the ‘‘Black Ship,’’ painted black from stem to stern and bearing no insignia to show its nationality. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Jan. 20, 1941', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/blogs/20iht-oldjan20-ship/20iht-oldjan20-ship-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/20/blogs/20iht-oldjan20-ship/20iht-oldjan20-ship-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/blogs/20iht-oldjan20-ship/20iht-oldjan20-ship-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/blogs/20iht-oldjan20-ship/20iht-oldjan20-ship-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/blogs/20iht-oldjan20-ship/20iht-oldjan20-ship-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/20/blogs/20iht-oldjan20-ship/20iht-oldjan20-ship-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/blogs/20iht-oldjan20-ship/20iht-oldjan20-ship-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1941: Nazi Raiders Reported Active', 'kicker': 'IHT