': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b96b86cf-c2a4-51f3-ae0a-b6a54647b73a', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b96b86cf-c2a4-51f3-ae0a-b6a54647b73a'}, {'abstract': 'Despite all the talk of over-treatment and high costs, end-of-life care in the United States isn’t as bad as you might think.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/20/opinion/is-it-better-to-die-in-america-or-in-england.html', 'snippet': 'Despite all the talk of over-treatment and high costs, end-of-life care in the United States isn’t as bad as you might think.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WE frequently hear complaints about how people near the end of life are treated in America. Patients are attached to tubes and machines and subjected to too many invasive procedures. Death occurs too frequently in the hospital, rather than at home, where the dying can be surrounded by loved ones. And it is way too expensive. Each year, the care of dying seniors consumes over 25 percent of Medicare expenditures.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/opinion/20emanuel/20emanuel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/20/opinion/20emanuel/20emanuel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/opinion/20emanuel/20emanuel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/opinion/20emanuel/20emanuel-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1266, 'width': 1899, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/opinion/20emanuel/20emanuel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/20/opinion/20emanuel/20emanuel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/opinion/20emanuel/20emanuel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is It Better to Die in America or in England?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is It Better to Die in America?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bekelman, Justin E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emanuel, Ezekiel J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Journal of the American Medical Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-19T15:59:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ezekiel J. Emanuel and Justin E. Bekelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ezekiel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Emanuel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Bekelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a7d0b8b-433b-53c5-823c-74272cabbf40', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a7d0b8b-433b-53c5-823c-74272cabbf40'}, {'abstract': 'The fire in house in Chillum , in Price George’s County, broke out in a home that appears to have had no smoke detectors, officials said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/20/us/twins-and-grandparents-killed-in-maryland-fire.html', 'snippet': 'The fire in house in Chillum , in Price George’s County, broke out in a home that appears to have had no smoke detectors, officials said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twin toddlers and their grandparents died in a fire in a suburban Maryland house early on Tuesday, and investigators said they were trying to determine the cause of the blaze. The home appears to have had no smoke detectors, the authorities said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twins and Grandparents Killed in Maryland Fire', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoke Detectors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': "Prince George's County (Md)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-19T16:12:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/180c37fe-7fb3-5457-8b7b-c69dd6589245', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/180c37fe-7fb3-5457-8b7b-c69dd6589245'}, {'abstract': 'A 5-year-old’s wail at a family party: “I don’t want YOUR daddy. Not Finley’s daddy, or Addie’s daddy, I want MY daddy.”', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/19/a-military-wife-during-deployment-is-asked-is-it-worth-it/', 'snippet': 'A 5-year-old’s wail at a family party: “I don’t want YOUR daddy. Not Finley’s daddy, or Addie’s daddy, I want MY daddy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'We sat around my sister’s dining room table, laughing so hard we had tears in our eyes, reliving and rehashing the events of the night before. My aunt and uncle “fishing” for each other on the dance floor. My brother’s hilarious toast to the bride and groom (my other brother). My cousin’s Russian girlfriend responding to someone’s question of “Where did he find you?” with “Amazon Prime.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/us/deploy-birthday-motherlode/deploy-birthday-motherlode-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/18/us/deploy-birthday-motherlode/deploy-birthday-motherlode-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/us/deploy-birthday-motherlode/deploy-birthday-motherlode-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 896, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/us/deploy-birthday-motherlode/deploy-birthday-motherlode-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 896, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/us/deploy-birthday-motherlode/deploy-birthday-motherlode-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/18/us/deploy-birthday-motherlode/deploy-birthday-motherlode-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/us/deploy-birthday-motherlode/deploy-birthday-motherlode-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Military Wife During Deployment Is Asked, ‘Is It Worth It?’', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-19T16:18:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By T. T. Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'T.', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f90722e-baf3-5b1f-ae90-b4638bea3201', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f90722e-baf3-5b1f-ae90-b4638bea3201'}, {'abstract': 'Freetown’s board houses are as old as the city itself, but they are fast disappearing amid rapid urbanization and a growing preference for concrete.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/20/world/africa/freetown-sierra-leone-board-houses.html', 'snippet': 'Freetown’s board houses are as old as the city itself, but they are fast disappearing amid rapid urbanization and a growing preference for concrete.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FREETOWN, Sierra Leone — On a busy roundabout in the heart of this nation’s capital stands an ancient cotton tree, marking the spot where Freetown was founded by freed slaves from North America more than 200 years ago. Walk for a few minutes toward the southeast, past the vendors who line the derelict remains of Victoria Park and through the bustling streets of the city center, and you will find at the corner of two rutted dirt roads a house that looks more suited to the American South than to a steamy West African capital.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/world/20SIERRALEONE-web1/20SIERRALEONE-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/20/world/20SIERRALEONE-web1/20SIERRALEONE-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/world/20SIERRALEONE-web1/20SIERRALEONE-web1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/world/20SIERRALEONE-web1/20SIERRALEONE-web1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/world/20SIERRALEONE-web1/20SIERR