idth': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/19/arts/20aiparis/20aiparis-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/19/arts/20aiparis/20aiparis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/19/arts/20aiparis/20aiparis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/19/arts/20aiparis/20aiparis-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ai Weiwei’s Latest Canvas: A Luxury Emporium in Paris', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ai Weiwei', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bon Marche (Paris, France, Retailer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-19T17:51:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Donadio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donadio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79665be0-3644-5f59-871a-76b0fa149300', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79665be0-3644-5f59-871a-76b0fa149300'}, {'abstract': 'A new collection of art essays and criticism will include more than 40 pieces that will be published for the first time, and some previously published only in limited editions.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/19/new-writings-by-donald-judd-coming-to-light/', 'snippet': 'A new collection of art essays and criticism will include more than 40 pieces that will be published for the first time, and some previously published only in limited editions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The artist Donald Judd, who died in 1994, remains known for many things – his sculpture, his spare architectural interventions, his exacting and obdurate personality. During his lifetime he was also known and widely revered for his writings – criticism and essays that took the measure of many of his contemporaries and helped set the terms for Minimalism, the movement he never quite claimed as his own. But for more than two decades, Judd’s collected writings, originally published in 1975, have been extremely hard to come by, with copies sometimes trading on used book sites for hundreds of dollars.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Writings by Donald Judd Coming to Light', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Publishing the Writings of Donald Judd', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Judd, Donald', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'March (San Francisco, Calif, Retail Store)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yellow Book', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zwirner, David, Gallery', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-19T17:52:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/490e66d6-2030-5e94-b01c-d73730f8d8fa', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/490e66d6-2030-5e94-b01c-d73730f8d8fa'}, {'abstract': 'Several men’s shows, including Prada, Etro and Vivienne Westwood, embraced a casual dishabille.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/19/t-magazine/fashion/gucci-prada-milan-mens-fashion-fall-2016.html', 'snippet': 'Several men’s shows, including Prada, Etro and Vivienne Westwood, embraced a casual dishabille.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Precise tailoring is usually the main attraction at the Milan men’s shows — but this season, a handful of designers have embraced a more undone look in their fall/winter 2016 collections. At Prada, plackets, collars and cuffs dangled from shirts and jackets, while at Gucci, Marni, Etro and Damir Doma, knits, trousers and coats came down the runway with frayed hems. And at Vivienne Westwood — certainly a patron saint of the unkempt — a patchwork sweater was cut open at the sleeves. It all makes one consider taking a pair of shears to one’s own clothes for next winter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/t-magazine/18tmag-dishabille-01/18tmag-dishabille-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/18/t-magazine/18tmag-dishabille-01/18tmag-dishabille-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/t-magazine/18tmag-dishabille-01/18tmag-dishabille-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/t-magazine/18tmag-dishabille-01/18tmag-dishabille-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/t-magazine/18tmag-dishabille-01/18tmag-dishabille-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/18/t-magazine/18tmag-dishabille-01/18tmag-dishabille-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/18/t-magazine/18tmag-dishabille-01/18tmag-dishabille-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Three’s a Trend: Men’s Wear, Come Undone', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Men's Fashion Week", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Prada SpA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Etro SpA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gucci', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marni Group', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Milan (Italy)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-19T17:55:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Fashion & Beauty', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Rider', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rider', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd2b5a34-d174-579f-b445-ccdc67149845', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd2b5a34-d174-579f-b445-ccdc67149845'}, {'abstract': 'The chief executive of the N.R.A. has challenged President Obama to a debate — but he sounds more like he’s calling for a duel.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/19/gun-debate-at-the-o-k-corral-or-somewhere/', 'snippet': 'The chief executive of the N.R.A. has challenged President Obama to a debate — but he sounds more like he’s calling for a duel.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The American people know a liar when they see one,” declared Wayne LaPierre, chief executive of the National Rifle Association, calling out President Obama for a one-on-one television debate on gun controls in a macho direct-address video that sounded more like a challenge to a duel. “Let’s see if you’re game for a fair debate,” said Mr. LaPierre. “It’s your chance to show the American people you’re not afraid to meet the NRA on neutral ground.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/19/opinion/19clinesWEB/19clinesWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/19/opinion/19clinesWEB/19clinesWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/19/opinion/19clinesWEB/19clinesWEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/19/opinion/19clines