ted', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f520c9a6-46a4-57be-818a-f94362eb9bdd', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f520c9a6-46a4-57be-818a-f94362eb9bdd'}, {'abstract': 'Rove’s account of William McKinley’s 1896 campaign presents him as an exemplary candidate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/books/review/the-triumph-of-william-mckinley-by-karl-rove.html', 'snippet': 'Rove’s account of William McKinley’s 1896 campaign presents him as an exemplary candidate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Writing six decades after the election of 1896 brought the retired Ohio governor William McKinley to the White House, the political scientist V.\u2009O. Key Jr. identified the Democratic defeat as “so demoralizing and so thorough that the party could make little headway in regrouping its forces until 1916.” The scale of this Republican success — a moment that ushered in a period of Republican Party ascendance that lasted until the election of Franklin Roosevelt in 1932 — draws the attention of the prominent political strategist Karl Rove, who also views McKinley’s victory over the charismatic William Jennings Bryan as pivotal. Rove is in awe, rightly so, of McKinley’s political achievement, and his richly detailed, moment-by-moment account in “The Triumph of William McKinley” brings to life the drama of an electoral contest whose outcome seemed uncertain to the candidate and his handlers until the end.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24KATZNELSON/24KATZNELSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24KATZNELSON/24KATZNELSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 973}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24KATZNELSON/24KATZNELSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 632, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24KATZNELSON/24KATZNELSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1233, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24KATZNELSON/24KATZNELSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24KATZNELSON/24KATZNELSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24KATZNELSON/24KATZNELSON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Triumph of William McKinley,’ by Karl Rove', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Model for Republicans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rove, Karl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKinley, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Triumph of William McKinley: Why the Election of 1896 Still Matters (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-20T10:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ira Katznelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ira', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Katznelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a45db3f-70fa-5315-8986-1bf55e50c399', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a45db3f-70fa-5315-8986-1bf55e50c399'}, {'abstract': 'How Americans came to perceive carefully composed images of their current and future presidents. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/books/review/david-greenbergs-republic-of-spin.html', 'snippet': 'How Americans came to perceive carefully composed images of their current and future presidents. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'When George Washington was president, our forebears did not wonder what spurred his occasional ghostwriter, Alexander Hamilton, to make a particular argument in the general’s 1796 farewell address. Nor did many voters in 1860 try to deconstruct why Abraham Lincoln’s propagandists chose to present him as a backwoods rail-splitter, which he had not been for a very long time, instead of the well-heeled corporate lawyer he had, thanks to hard work and talent, become.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24BESCHLOSS/24BESCHLOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 596, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24BESCHLOSS/24BESCHLOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 596}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24BESCHLOSS/24BESCHLOSS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1016, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'im