and Gasoline', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-20T11:55:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Ives', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ives', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aebdab61-a00b-5908-9add-5427ba979f22', 'word_count': 976, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aebdab61-a00b-5908-9add-5427ba979f22'}, {'abstract': 'Invoking an act of God to explain a human-made tragedy has become the go-to excuse in public life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/magazine/how-the-perfect-storm-became-the-perfect-cop-out.html', 'snippet': 'Invoking an act of God to explain a human-made tragedy has become the go-to excuse in public life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a grand jury decided late last month not to indict the two police officers involved in the fatal shooting of Tamir Rice, the 12-year-old whose killing outside a Cleveland recreation center became a cause célèbre of the Black Lives Matter movement, the task of announcing the verdict fell to the Cuyahoga County prosecutor Timothy J. McGinty. McGinty, who is in the midst of a heated Democratic primary race, appeared decidedly unhappy to be handling the case. When he presented himself to the TV cameras on Dec. 28, he shifted from foot to foot and often looked fixedly at his prepared statement. He said he told Rice’s mother that what happened to her son was ‘‘undeniably tragic.’’ Nevertheless, he went on, ‘‘Given this perfect storm of human error, mistakes and miscommunication by all involved that day, the evidence did not indicate criminal conduct by police.’’', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24firstwords/24mag-24firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24firstwords/24mag-24firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 592}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24firstwords/24mag-24firstwords-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1011, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24firstwords/24mag-24firstwords-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1772, 'width': 1795, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24firstwords/24mag-24firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24firstwords/24mag-24firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24firstwords/24mag-24firstwords-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the ‘Perfect Storm’ Became the Perfect Cop-Out', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cloud Cover', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Homans, Charles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cleveland (Ohio)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Tamir E (2002-14)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Enron Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-20T12:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Homans', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Homans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bec9862-0116-5314-8804-7517eb614879', 'word_count': 1550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bec9862-0116-5314-8804-7517eb614879'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s ethicist on a sibling’s struggle, favors in the workplace and secrets between friends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/magazine/should-i-help-my-sister-end-her-life.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s ethicist on a sibling’s struggle, favors in the workplace and secrets between friends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have a 50-year-old sibling with multiple medical conditions (uncontrolled epilepsy, a stroke that left her physically and mentally impaired, paranoid schizophrenia, to name a few) that have left her isolated and miserable. My 80-plus-year-old parents are her caretakers. She has the very best doctors, social workers and therapists — but none can give her a fulfilling life. She calls me crying every night, often threatening to commit suicide, a threat she has tried to make good on dozens of times. I’m struggling to know if I should stop talking her out of suicide and instead give her options for a death with dignity. If she were dying of a terminal illness, the discussion could be more frank. But what of these chronic conditions with no end in sight? Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24ethicist/24ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 386, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24ethicist/24ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 386, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24ethicist/24ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24ethicist/24ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24ethicist/24ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should I Help My Sister End Her Life?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Sister Wants to End Her Life. Should I Help?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appiah, Kwame Anthony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicists, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Euthanasia and Assisted Suicide', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-20T12:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca386a33-6d8d-592e-b608-422ce55f7e25', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca386a33-6d8d-592e-b608-422ce55f7e25'}, {'abstract': 'The venture capital firm points to growth in enterprise software companies and health care information technology businesses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/21/business/dealbook/norwest-venture-partners-raises-a-new-1-2-billion-fund.html', 'snippet': 'The venture capital firm points to growth in enterprise software companies and health care information technology businesses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Silicon Valley may be in a state of what could be described diplomatically as nervous. But one venture capital firm is still betting that it can profitably put a huge amount of capital to work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Norwest Venture Partners Raises a New $1.2 Billion Fund', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Enterprise Computing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Norwest Venture Partners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-20T12:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d1e63c7-7994-55d2-b84a-3c83a2c71f96', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d1e63c7-7994-55d2-b84a-3c83a2c71f96'}, {'abstract': "It's an economic mystery — but it might tell us something important about how C.E.O.s and investors think about the future.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/magazine/why-are-corporations-hoarding-trillions.html', 'snippet': "It's an economic mystery — but it might tell us something important about how C.E.O.s and investors think about the future.", 'lead_paragraph': 'There is an economic mystery I’ve been struggling to understand for quite some time, and I’m not the only one who’s confused: Among financial experts, it is often referred to as a conundrum, a paradox, a puzzle. The mystery is as follows: Collectively, American businesses currently have $1.9 trillion in cash, just sitting around. Not only is this state of affairs unparalleled in economic history, but we don’t even have much data to compare it with, because corporations have traditionally been borrowers, not savers. The notion that a corporation would hold on to so much of its profit seems economically absurd, especially now, when it is probably earning only about 2 percent interest by parking that money in United States Treasury bonds. These companies would be better off investing in anything — a product, a service, a corporate acquisition — that would make them more than 2 cents of profit on the dollar, a razor-thin margin by corporate standards. And yet they choose to keep the cash.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24onmoney1/24mag-24onmoney-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 515, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24onmoney1/24mag-24onmoney-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 515}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24onmoney1/24mag-24onmoney-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 878, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24onmoney1/24mag-24onmoney-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1352, 'width': 1576, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24onmoney1/24onmoney1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24onmoney1/24onmoney1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/magazine/24onmoney1/24onmoney1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Are Corporations Hoarding Trillions?', 'kicker': 'On Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Are Corporations Hoarding Trillions?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Savings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alphabet Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-20T12:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Davidson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davidson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a5ea054-c8ec-5fad-9840-bf0968149f0d', 'word_count': 1354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a5ea054-c8ec-5fad-9840-bf0968149f0d'}, {'abstract': 'The smallest bits of news can bring a wave of coverage that reshapes the race at a crucial stage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/21/upshot/a-kasich-surge-even-a-suspect-poll-can-help-this-time-of-year.html', 'snippet': 'The smallest bits of news can bring a wave of coverage that reshapes the race at a crucial stage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The final couple of weeks before Iowa and New Hampshire are often among the most volatile of the primary season. The candidates start attacking one another, voters start making up their minds, and even the smallest shifts can bring a wave of media coverage that reshapes the race at a crucial stage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/upshot/21UP-Kasich/21UP-Kasich-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/20/upshot/21UP-Kasich/21UP-Kasich-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/20/upshot/21UP-Kasich/21UP-Kasich-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'le