e': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d251c354-f68f-5263-9cbf-e59b854fab67', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d251c354-f68f-5263-9cbf-e59b854fab67'}, {'abstract': 'In two charming new picture books, the abominable snowman has been entirely defanged.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/21/books/review/dear-yeti-and-the-thing-about-yetis.html', 'snippet': 'In two charming new picture books, the abominable snowman has been entirely defanged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was growing up, the Yeti — who back then went by the more formal and forbidding name “abominable snowman” — was a creature capable of stirring up serious fear. Like the Loch Ness monster, this gigantic people-eating being actually existed, we children knew. There were, predictably, adult doubters. That made the question of whether he was merely mythical a topic every curious child had to investigate. My own research resulted in a habit of mind I’ve carried with me all these years, swinging between a creeping horror at all the unsettling supernatural realities most adults are too thick-headed to even acknowledge, and that feeling that comes after a good debunking — a mixture of emotional comfort, intellectual pleasure and condescending pity aimed at the still benighted myth-believers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/21/books/review/21childrens-2/21childrens-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 240, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/21/books/review/21childrens-2/21childrens-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 240}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'c