k': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-Time (Play)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Abuse, Rights and Welfare', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-22T10:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7ea60e8-7858-5534-80d0-4d27c32b8f74', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7ea60e8-7858-5534-80d0-4d27c32b8f74'}, {'abstract': 'Four siblings reunite to decide the fate of the family’s crumbling rectory.  ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/books/review/the-past-by-tessa-hadley.html', 'snippet': 'Four siblings reunite to decide the fate of the family’s crumbling rectory.  ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pity poor Roland Crane, one of the central characters in Tessa Hadley’s most recent novel, “The Past.” Like other males who populate Hadley’s fiction, Roland grew up in a family of vivid, sexually flamboyant women who considered him “slightly ridiculous.” Today, however, he is a public intellectual, with a gratifying position writing about film for the national newspapers. When he shows up for a summer holiday with his three grown sisters, driving a vintage Jaguar and wearing a white linen suit, accompanied by his glamorous new wife and his lovely 16-year-old daughter by a previous marriage, you might expect Roland to have earned a little domestic peace and adulation. But no. Not only do his sisters, who “could remember him in short trousers, when his \xadglasses were mended with sticking plaster,” still consider him a \xad“buttoned-up” puritan, but his sway over his wife and daughter is about to be assailed from the most unexpected quarters. The point about the past, in Hadley’s fiction, is that it’s always there to trip you up. Your parents (whether dead or alive), your siblings (whether successful or resentfully needy) will inevitably threaten to suck you back into that sticky-sweet limbo of childhood myth and trauma.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/0124-BKS-Eberstadt/0124-BKS-Eberstadt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 845, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/0124-BKS-Eberstadt/0124-BKS-Eberstadt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 845}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/0124-BKS-Eberstadt/0124-BKS-Eberstadt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 727, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/0124-BKS-Eberstadt/0124-BKS-Eberstadt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/0124-BKS-Eberstadt/0124-BKS-Eberstadt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/0124-BKS-Eberstadt/0124-BKS-Eberstadt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/0124-BKS-Eberstadt/0124-BKS-Eberstadt-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarg