he residents of a subterranean apartment building.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jim Krusoe’s sixth novel, “The Sleep Garden,” is a labyrinth of different narratives. Or, to be exact, a burrow — the Burrow, as Krusoe calls the surreal underground apartment building where his five central characters dwell (part reality show setup, part Plato’s cave, part grave) in a state of stasis and frustrated ambition. Their aspirations — for sex, love, money, celebrity — are familiar, though their milieu is not: the claustrophobic Burrow, filled with mirrors that “multiply the dark.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24PHILLIPS/0124-BKS-Phillips-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24PHILLIPS/0124-BKS-Phillips-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24PHILLIPS/0124-BKS-Phillips-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24PHILLIPS/0124-BKS-Phillips-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1097, 'width': 1461, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24PHILLIPS/24PHILLIPS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/24PHILLIPS/24PHILLIPS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge