'persons', 'value': 'Loewe SA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-22T12:14:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Men’s Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Emmrich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Emmrich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d6491ed-04f8-5156-9bf9-a01cbe22e3f2', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d6491ed-04f8-5156-9bf9-a01cbe22e3f2'}, {'abstract': 'Dr. Lucy Kalanithi made sure Paul’s final thoughts led to the book “When Breath Becomes Air,” and has continued his conversation on living and dying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/26/health/lucy-paul-kalanithi-interview-breath-becomes-air.html', 'snippet': 'Dr. Lucy Kalanithi made sure Paul’s final thoughts led to the book “When Breath Becomes Air,” and has continued his conversation on living and dying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On March 9, 2015, two years after learning he had metastatic lung cancer, Dr. Paul Kalanithi, 37, a neurosurgeon at Stanford University, died. He left behind his wife, Dr. Lucy Kalanithi, their 8-month-old daughter, Cady, and a file on his computer labeled Kalanithi-Ms-20Feb.docx. His wife took that document, shepherded its contents to publication, and the resulting book, “When Breath Becomes Air,” became an instant best-seller this month. Dr. Kalanithi, 36, is a general internist at Stanford, where she helps lead efforts to improve the quality and efficiency of care. She recently folded herself into a leather armchair in her home in the San Francisco Bay Area, the same spot where her husband sat while he wrote, and talked about her own experience — as a young widow in grief, as her husband’s literary stand-in, and as a physician who witnessed at intensely personal range the end to an extraordinary man’s life. Here are edited excerpts from the conversation.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/22/science/26QNA/26QNA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/22/science/26QNA/26QNA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/22/science/26QNA/26QNA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/22/science/26QNA/26QNA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/22/science/26QNA/26QNA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/22/science/26QNA/26QNA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/22/science/26QNA/26QNA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping Dr. Paul Kalanithi’s Voice Alive', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keeping a Husband’s Voice Alive', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hafner, Katie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kalanithi, Lucy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kalanithi, Paul (1977-2015)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lung Cancer', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'When Breath Becomes Air (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-22T12:15:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Hafner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hafner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/2780003f-9258-58eb-81fa-de2e9b178ac3', 'word_count': 1510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2780003f-9258-58eb-81fa-de2e9b178ac3'}, {'abstract': 'Trapped in Spain’s recession, this novel’s protagonist embodies the effects of his country’s corruption.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/books/review/on-the-edge-by-rafael-chirbes.html', 'snippet': 'Trapped in Spain’s recession, this novel’s protagonist embodies the effects of his country’s corruption.', 'lead_paragraph': '“On the Edge” is not a book you want to read in fits and starts. It is an anti-tweet, a brick of dense prose, that 70-year-old uncle who corners you at a holiday party, grabs you by the lapels and demands you hear him out. Your eyes sometimes glaze over, and you occasionally have to wipe a fleck of whitish spit off your face, but once you give yourself over to his story, you find there are plenty of rewards. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/books/review/