 Shifted After Last Year&#8217;s Security Breach ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Julie Hirschfeld', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberwarfare and Defense', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of Personnel Management', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of Management and Budget (US)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-23T00:49:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Hirschfeld Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': 'Hirschfeld', 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c76f5242-b0cf-5b20-97b6-89a06e37247b', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c76f5242-b0cf-5b20-97b6-89a06e37247b'}, {'abstract': 'A snowstorm rewards indolence and punishes go-getters, which is why it’s the best natural disaster there is.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/23/opinion/in-case-of-blizzard-do-nothing.html', 'snippet': 'A snowstorm rewards indolence and punishes go-getters, which is why it’s the best natural disaster there is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the winter of 1985 my hometown, Buffalo, was engulfed in a blizzard — not an uncommon occurrence for the region, which is justly famed for epic snows. But this was a big one, and the city’s blustery Irish-American mayor, Jimmy Griffin, was at pains to persuade people to stop trying to go about their business as conditions deteriorated. He urged Buffalonians to “relax, stay inside and grab a six-pack,” which must be the best advice any elected official ever gave the public in an emergency situation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/23/opinion/23dudley/23dudley-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/23/opinion/23dudley/23dudley-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/23/opinion/23dudley/23dudley-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/23/opinion/23dudley/23dudley-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1432, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/23/opinion/23dudley/23dudley-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/23/opinion/23dudley/23dudley-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/23/opinion/23dudley/23dudley-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Case of Blizzard, Do Nothing', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Case of Blizzard, Do Nothing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Buffalo (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-23T00:53:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Dudley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dudley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d7e41fa-9cdd-5a3b-823d-60c389d746ef', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d7e41fa-9cdd-5a3b-823d-60c389d746ef'}, {'abstract': 'Why the radio silence.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/22/on-the-road-personal-and-trivial/', 'snippet': 'Why the radio silence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just a note on radio silence: I’ve been traveling — back home yesterday, and then basically fleeing the New York area, moving up a family-related car trip so as not to get trapped by snow. Destination safely reached, but bandwidth very limited, so no posts, musical or otherwise, today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On The Road (Personal and Trivial)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-01-23T00:54:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c26fc712-d447-5d08-820c-2f6f4acb7ded', 'word_count': 49, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c26fc712-d447-5d08-820c-2f6f4acb7ded'}, {'abstract': 'Many of the gardens occupy plots of land owned by the City of New York, leaving residents who have tended them for years with few options to oppose the changes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/23/nyregion/community-gardens-imperiled-by-new-yorksaffordable-housing-plans.html', 'snippet': 'Many of the gardens occupy plots of land owned by the City of New York, leaving residents who have tended them for years with few options to oppose the changes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apart from a colorful hand-painted sign, there is little to suggest that the Mandela Community Garden in Harlem, a lot piled with pieces of pavement and bounded by graffitied walls and a chain-link fence, is a garden at all. The volunteers who pulled up the concrete had made plans for the spring, however: to pepper the dirt with wildflower seeds, make sculptures from the rubble and turn the lot into a verdant little oasis.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/23/nyregion/23GARDENS1/23GARDENS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/23/nyregion/23GARDENS1/23GARDENS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/23/nyregion/23GARDENS1/23GARDENS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima