r Service said 26.8 inches of snow had fallen on Central Park by midnight, making this the city’s second-biggest snowstorm since 1869.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Making up for a remarkably mild winter, the first major snowstorm of the season charged up the East Coast on Saturday, a blizzard propelled by tropical-storm-force winds that brought much of the Northeast to a standstill and left more than two feet of snow in New York City, where Mayor Bill de Blasio called it “very likely one of the worst snowstorms in our history.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Major Cities Grind to a Halt and Face Days of Digging Out Snow', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Swirling Storm Draws White Curtain Over East', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rojas, Rick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Coast (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-23T13:32:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron and Rick Rojas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rojas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd2e8dee-f070-54a1-859d-7cf620500219', 'word_count': 1816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd2e8dee-f070-54a1-859d-7cf620500219'}, {'abstract': 'New! Discuss the “A Little Variety” puzzles here.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/23/a-little-variety-we-welcome-your-comments/', 'snippet': 'New! Discuss the “A Little Variety” puzzles here.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A LITTLE VARIETY — We’re trying something new — a weekly place to gather and discuss the “A Little Variety” puzzles that publish online on Thursdays and appear in the Sunday Magazine. You are welcome to talk about the puzzles in the comments section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "‘A Little Variety': We Welcome Your Comments", 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-23T14:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a547cb8d-b151-5e5e-921d-604fb60be8da', 'word_count': 93, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a547cb8d-b151-5e5e-921d-604fb60be8da'}, {'abstract': 'News flash: progressive can honestly disagree with your candidate’s positions.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/23/wonks-and-minions/', 'snippet': 'News flash: progressive can honestly disagree with your candidate’s positions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the differences between right and left in America is that the progressive infrastructure includes a contingent of genuine wonks — commentators on policy who really do make models and crunch numbers, and sometimes come up with answers that aren’t fully predictable from their politics. The list includes Ezra Klein, Jonathan Cohn, Jonathan Chait, Mike Konczal, myself some of the time, and others. Right now the wonk brigade has been weighing in on Bernie Sanders, and is in general not too impressed on either financial reform or health care.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wonks and Minions', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-01-23T14:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5594ad24-846b-5642-90d7-4b706eed8ecf', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5594ad24-846b-5642-90d7-4b706eed8ecf'}, {'abstract': 'The future of messaging apps may be as a catchall for shopping, booking rides, talking to customer service agents and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/technology/farhad-and-mikes-week-in-tech-the-chatter-on-messaging-apps.html', 'snippet': 'The future of messaging apps may be as a catchall for shopping, booking rides, talking to customer service agents and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each Saturday, Farhad Manjoo and Mike Isaac, technology reporters at The New York Times, review the week’s news, offering analysis and maybe a joke or two about the most important developments in the tech industry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/24/business/24technewsletter-web1/24technewsletter-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/24/business/24technewsletter-web1/24technewsletter-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit':