: 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Freehill-Maye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Freehill-Maye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e598861-2b16-5810-bc79-6eb4f764fee7', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e598861-2b16-5810-bc79-6eb4f764fee7'}, {'abstract': 'The suffix has become a way to call bigotry a disease — and to antagonize ideological opponents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/magazine/how-phobic-became-a-weapon-in-the-identity-wars.html', 'snippet': 'The suffix has become a way to call bigotry a disease — and to antagonize ideological opponents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It started, innocently enough, at Hooters. A waitress who goes by Zola got to talking with a diner named Jessica. The two bonded over a shared romantic philosophy (each had a ‘‘sugar daddy’’ at home) and career focus (each moonlighted as an exotic dancer), and when Jessica invited Zola on a road trip to Florida to strip at some local clubs, Zola strapped in. Little did Zola know, she was hurtling toward the center of a backwater psychodrama in which she would face off against a series of pimps, johns, kidnappers, one very weepy white boy and Jessica herself, who had pulled her new friend into this nonconsensual weekend tour of the underground sex trade.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31firstwords/31mag-31firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31firstwords/31mag-31firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption'