, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1c987e1-eb89-5ae7-bd2d-4eb47db22b41', 'word_count': 1686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1c987e1-eb89-5ae7-bd2d-4eb47db22b41'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Spain plans to intern German troops in Guinea in 1916.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/26/1916-spain-to-intern-german-troops/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Spain plans to intern German troops in Guinea in 1916.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The British successes in Cameroon are likely to be of indirect consequence to this country. In December last the Spanish Government, wishing to prevent any possible breach of Spain’s neutrality, entered into correspondence with the German, French and British Governments. Even at that time it appeared evident that the Germans would eventually be defeated in Cameroon, and Spain insisted upon a German assurance that, should the German troops be forced to take refuge in Spanish territory at Guinea, they would consent to be disarmed and interned. After lengthy negotiations, Germany reluctantly gave this assurance, with a rider to the effect that a German defeat was ‘‘impossible.’’ — The New York Herald, European Edition, Jan. 27, 1916', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/27/blogs/27iht-oldjan27-guinea/27iht-oldjan27-guinea-jumbo.png', 'height': 695, 'width': 581, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/27/blogs/27iht-oldjan27-guinea/27iht-oldjan27-guinea-superJumbo.png', 'height': 695, 'width': 581, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype