': 'organizations', 'value': "Danish People's Party", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-26T18:36:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Bilefsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilefsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5da9ed2-2959-500b-9d84-f9c9ab3bdd39', 'word_count': 736, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5da9ed2-2959-500b-9d84-f9c9ab3bdd39'}, {'abstract': 'Also, Alain Coumont opens Le Botaniste, a vegetarian wine bar, on the Upper East Side, and Claus Meyer is presenting a pop-up bakery in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/27/dining/poke-restaurant-wisefish-chelsea.html', 'snippet': 'Also, Alain Coumont opens Le Botaniste, a vegetarian wine bar, on the Upper East Side, and Claus Meyer is presenting a pop-up bakery in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wisefish Poké Not that restaurants are any more reliable than finance, but Drew Crane and Bryan Cowan have fled the world of money management to bring their passion for poké (poh-kay), the Hawaiian specialty they encountered in the islands, to New York. “We love poké and saw that it has exploded on the West Coast,” Mr. Crane said. “We want to make it acceptable here.” (Actually, New York now has a number of poké options.) The dish is simple: raw fish, often tuna, diced in a bowl with rice, garnishes and dressings. And it is well suited to “fast casual” dining; bowls, in three sizes, are assembled at a counter according to set combinations or the customer’s choice of ingredients: 263 West 19th Street, 212-367-7653, wisefishpoke.com. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank':