page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vietnam’s Communist Party Gives Old-Guard Leader a New 5-Year Term', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Term for Leader in Vietnam', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trong, Nguyen Phu', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of Vietnam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dung, Nguyen Tan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South China Sea', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trans-Pacific Partnership', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-27T10:20:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Ives', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ives', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7071bafe-4cb5-5005-bd89-baea85f80f37', 'word_count': 772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7071bafe-4cb5-5005-bd89-baea85f80f37'}, {'abstract': 'Twenty years after “Notes From a Small Island,” Bryson again takes stock of his adopted country, Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/books/review/bill-bryson-the-road-to-little-dribbling-review.html', 'snippet': 'Twenty years after “Notes From a Small Island,” Bryson again takes stock of his adopted country, Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I watched the rain beat down on the road outside and told myself that one day this would be 20 years ago.” Why on earth would the weary traveler who consoled himself with this notion, marooned in a shuttered-tight, sodden Welsh mining town, even consider hitting the road again? Anyone who followed Bill Bryson on his trek around Britain in “Notes From a Small Island,” published here exactly 20 years ago, will instantly understand. From the very beginning, Bryson, an American from Iowa who has lived and worked and established a family on the far side of the Atlantic, has responded to his British surroundings with an irresistible mix of frustration and fascination. That first travelogue was inspired by what turned out to be a temporary move back to the United States. This new one, which begins with Bryson taking a challenging (and, he mischievously observes, not entirely accurate) test to qualify for dual citizenship, has him questioning how much he really understands modern Britain, “a country that I don’t altogether recognize.” Has he just become older and crankier? Or have the places he first knew as a young man really changed? There’s a simple way to find out.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31BECKER/31BECKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31BECKER/31BECKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31BECKER/31BECKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31BECKER/31BECKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31BECKER/31BECKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31BECKER/31BECKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31BECKER/31BECKER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill Bryson’s ‘The Road to Little Dribbling’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Walk in the Wolds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bryson, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Road to Little Dribbling: Adventures of an American in Britain (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-27T10:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alida Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/20b959e3-e1ee-56fa-ae68-af65751c8bbd', 'word_count': 1166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20b959e3-e1ee-56fa-ae68-af65751c8bbd'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books address slow economic growth from different perspectives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/books/review/the-age-of-stagnation-and-the-only-game-in-town.html', 'snippet': 'Two new books address slow economic growth from different perspectives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even more than income inequality, slow growth sits firmly atop the leader board of global economic worries. Among the most prosperous countries — the Group of 7 — not a single nation is achieving an acceptable rate of expansion. Once formidable emerging countries are in disarray: Brazil and Russia have fallen into deep recessions, and China is, at best, decelerating; only India emits a soupçon of positive energy.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31RATTNER02/31RATTNER02-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 484, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31RATTNER02/31RATTNER02-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 484}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31RATTNER02/31RATTNER02-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 827, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31RATTNER02/31RATTNER02-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1653, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31RATTNER02/31RATTNER02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31RATTNER02/31RATTNER02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 's