terial': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/6afaed61-6289-51e8-bee8-fe6890924d53', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/6afaed61-6289-51e8-bee8-fe6890924d53'}, {'abstract': 'The support is concentrated in Florida, the Northeast and across much of the West.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/27/upshot/where-mainstream-republican-candidates-are-most-popular.html', 'snippet': 'The support is concentrated in Florida, the Northeast and across much of the West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even as many Republican voters flirt with outsider candidates, a large minority of Republicans still supports one of the mainstream, traditional conservatives: Marco Rubio, Jeb Bush, Chris Christie, John Kasich and Carly Fiorina.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'We’ve Mapped Where Mainstream G.O.P. Candidates Are Most Popular (It’s Not the South)', 'kicker': 'The 2016 Race', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubio, Marco', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Civis Analytics Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-27T11:57:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/685bba31-c897-5adb-a501-e50140dd02c1', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/685bba31-c897-5adb-a501-e50140dd02c1'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on how we define race and whether you should disclose a friend’s struggle with depression.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/magazine/can-i-call-my-nonbiological-twins-black-because-my-husband-is.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on how we define race and whether you should disclose a friend’s struggle with depression.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m a Caucasian woman married to an African-American man. Shortly after we married, I discovered that I couldn’t conceive my own biological children. We opted to ‘‘adopt’’ two embryos. (Couples who have successfully undergone in-vitro fertilization and don’t wish to have more children can donate remaining embryos to other couples.) I was soon pregnant and gave birth to twins. Based on the records of the fertility clinic, we know that our children are genetically mixed Hispanic and Caucasian. I am not comfortable being open about the origin of my children, except with family and close friends, until they are old enough for me to explain it to them. However, several times in the last three years, I’ve been asked about their race, most recently on a pre-K school application form. On this form, there is no option of ‘‘mixed race’’ or ‘‘other.’’ Therefore, I identified my children as black. Was this the right choice? Name Withheld, Chicago', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31ethicist/31ethicist-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 299, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31ethicist/31ethicist-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 299}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31ethicist/31ethicist-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31ethicist/31ethicist-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1020, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31ethicist/31ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31ethicist/31ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31ethicist/31ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can I Call My Nonbiological Twins Black Because My Husband Is?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can I Call My Non-Biological Twins Black Because My Husband Is?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appiah, Kwame Anthony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicists, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-27T12:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname':