eens, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chocolate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Valentine's Day", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-27T18:13:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jaime Joyce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jaime', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joyce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc173096-b6bb-56df-b93e-9f762bfeb31f', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc173096-b6bb-56df-b93e-9f762bfeb31f'}, {'abstract': 'The English rapper stars in “Queen’s Speech,” a popular series of five videos in which she administers crisp put-downs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/arts/music/lady-leshurr-brings-her-haughty-english-rap-to-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'The English rapper stars in “Queen’s Speech,” a popular series of five videos in which she administers crisp put-downs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s Lady Leshurr, walking toward the camera through single, continuous takes in her series of five videos, “Queen’s Speech,” collectively watched more than 37 million times last year. A deft rapper from Birmingham, England, real name Melesha O’Garro, Lady Leshurr keeps a conversational, demure tone as she administers put-downs and Internet meme jokes to the camera; crisp and haughty, she cracks on your hygiene, your friends, your whole world. (“I can’t stand girls who take their heels off when they’re in a rave,” she raps in Episode 3. “I’ll step on your big toe, just to remind you how to behave.”) Grime — English rap — remains fairly obscure in the United States, despite flickers of interest from Kanye West and others. (Last August, Erykah Badu put up Episode 3 on her Facebook page with the message “ILL.”) But Lady Leshurr, making mixtapes since 2009, is packaged and ready. She makes her New York debut at the Gramercy Theater at 8 p.m. on Saturday, Feb. 6. (venue.thegramercytheatre.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE2/31GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE2/31GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE2/31GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE2/31GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE2/31GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE2/31GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE2/31GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lady Leshurr Brings Her Haughty English Rap to New York', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop; Exporting a Voice, Crisp and Haughty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lady Leshurr (Rapper)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-27T18:13:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f35bea7f-514f-5718-8ae9-e1e76116df48', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f35bea7f-514f-5718-8ae9-e1e76116df48'}, {'abstract': 'The Jerusalem, Miró, Orion, Escher and Danish string quartets perform this cycle at the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center through Feb. 21.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/arts/music/beethoven-string-quartet-cycle-at-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': 'The Jerusalem, Miró, Orion, Escher and Danish string quartets perform this cycle at the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center through Feb. 21.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While it’s hard to argue that the world — especially the little slice of it known as New York City — needs another Beethoven string quartet cycle, only the most churlish will be able to resist the draw of the one opening this week at the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center. For the encyclopedically minded, the chronological order will be reassuring. For those craving variety, there’s the appeal of watching five different ensembles tackle this marathon in relay formation.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE6/31GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE6/31GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty