ca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a4af615-8a3c-5d51-a9c2-a1f918ee1011', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a4af615-8a3c-5d51-a9c2-a1f918ee1011'}, {'abstract': 'The South African choreographer Dada Masilo tinkers with sexual convention in her version of this ballet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/arts/dance/a-radical-reinterpretation-of-swan-lake.html', 'snippet': 'The South African choreographer Dada Masilo tinkers with sexual convention in her version of this ballet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Choreographers keep peering into “Swan Lake,” searching for new themes in its symbolic waters. The latest to do so is Dada Masilo of South Africa, whose “Swan Lake” combines Tchaikovsky’s old ballet with modern realities. Although Ms. Masilo’s version, to a collage combining Tchaikovsky and contemporary music, establishes a carnival atmosphere and satirizes balletic conventions, it focuses on sexual politics.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE8/31GUIDE8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE8/31GUIDE8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE8/31GUIDE8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/arts/31GUIDE8/31GUIDE8-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 988, 'width': 1595, 'su