com/2016/01/27/t-magazine/art/drawing-then-1960s-art-dominique-levy.html', 'snippet': 'A Dominique Lévy Gallery exhibition builds on a seminal 1976 MoMA show.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Starting in the late ’60s, I think that drawing took a new form,” says the abstract painter Dorothea Rockburne. “For the most part, but not always — certainly not with Matisse — drawing had been a way of pre-thinking the structure in a painting. But drawing is not the orphan child of painting; it is a deep, material way of working.” Rockburne eyes the catalogue for the Museum of Modern Art’s 1976 exhibition “Drawing Now,” organized by Bernice Rose, folded over the armrest of her couch. “Drawing is exquisite, and Bernice picked up on that in her show, and Kate did, too.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/27/t-magazine/27tmag-levy1/27tmag-levy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 283, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/27/t-magazine/27tmag-levy1/27tmag-levy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 283}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/27/t-magazine/27tmag-levy1/27tmag-levy1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 483, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/27/t-magazine/27tmag-levy1/27tmag-levy1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 966, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/27/t-magazine/27tmag-levy1/27tmag-levy1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/27/t-magazin