 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikos Konstandaras', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikos', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Konstandaras', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd612c70-bdfe-54aa-a72f-ad37f5a478b1', 'word_count': 1105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd612c70-bdfe-54aa-a72f-ad37f5a478b1'}, {'abstract': 'As alarm grows over Zika, airlines are allowing passengers to change flights or receive refunds, and some tour companies are seeing cancellations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/29/travel/how-the-zika-virus-is-affecting-travel.html', 'snippet': 'As alarm grows over Zika, airlines are allowing passengers to change flights or receive refunds, and some tour companies are seeing cancellations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The travel industry is beginning to react as vacationers rethink their trips amid growing concern over the Zika virus, the mosquito-borne disease that experts say is possibly linked to microcephaly in babies. The spread of the virus has led the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention to warn pregnant women and those planning to become pregnant against travel to affected areas, including popular Caribbean tourist destinations like Puerto Rico, the United States Virgin Islands and Barbados, as well as Mexico, Brazil and Panama.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/29/travel/29intransitphoto-zikaperu/29intransitphoto-zikaperu-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/29/travel/29intransitphoto-zikaperu/29intransitphoto-zikaperu-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i