ame': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f2b591a-f210-58a4-8e72-fff54bd2dffa', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f2b591a-f210-58a4-8e72-fff54bd2dffa'}, {'abstract': 'Drawing inspiration at the bar from a great painter of the Harlem Renaissance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/magazine/drinking-with-archibald-motley.html', 'snippet': 'Drawing inspiration at the bar from a great painter of the Harlem Renaissance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last fall, I went to the Whitney Museum of American Art to see the exhibition ‘‘Archibald Motley: Jazz Age Modernist.’’ I found myself spellbound by a nightclub scene: a vivid tableau of dancing couples, so close to the band — a trombone’s slide stretches out between three people — as to appear in a tangle. Another couple can be glimpsed seated at a table, a glass of wine in front of the woman and a cocktail (it looks like a Manhattan) set before her date. I was surprised to see that Motley named the painting ‘‘Blues.’’ The heavy-souled sadness I associate with that music seems at odds with the image — but Motley must have known it was in there, too.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31drink/31mag-31drink-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31drink/31mag-31drink-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 801}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/magazine/31drink/31mag-31drink-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': 