st.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T15:52:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd51a660-d5d9-5d21-90ea-c0506c6f9fbc', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd51a660-d5d9-5d21-90ea-c0506c6f9fbc'}, {'abstract': 'Jane Mayer’s “Dark Money,” No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has made news partly because it seems Mayer was investigated by the book’s subjects, the Koch brothers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/02/07/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Jane Mayer’s “Dark Money,” No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has made news partly because it seems Mayer was investigated by the book’s subjects, the Koch brothers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Attack the Messenger: Jane Mayer’s “Dark Money,” about the influence of wealthy families in conservative politics, hits the hardcover nonfiction list at No.\u202f5. Mayer’s book has made news partly for its revelation that even as she was investigating the Koch brothers, they seemed to be investigating her: “Dirt, dirt, dirt,” one source told Mayer, describing the mission a team of operatives had to smear her name after The New Yorker published her first long article about the Kochs’ political efforts. (Mayer goes where the facts take her: She had previously written a similar article about George Soros’s influence on liberal causes.) “It’s uncommon for a private detective to be hired to conduct a retaliatory investigation into a reporter’s character,” she writes in the book, adding that although it’s unclear how directly involved they were, “clues leading back to the Kochs were everywhere.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/07/books/review/07mayer/07mayer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/07/books/review/07mayer/07mayer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/07/books/review/07mayer/07mayer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/02/07/books/review/07mayer/07mayer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/07/books/review/07mayer/07mayer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T15:54:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8a05d5c-51f9-5da9-91f0-0e380e298afd', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8a05d5c-51f9-5da9-91f0-0e380e298afd'}, {'abstract': 'According to Guinness World Records, the most prolific author as measured by words published is Charles Harold St. John Hamilton, with 72 to 73 million words to his 25 pen names.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/books/review/more-is-more-more-more.html', 'snippet': 'According to Guinness World Records, the most prolific author as measured by words published is Charles Harold St. John Hamilton, with 72 to 73 million words to his 25 pen names.', 'lead_paragraph': 'January is the month of resolutions, and in her Help Desk column this week, Molly Young tackles new books that promise to show us how to become our most productive selves. Writers — often known in our romantic parlance for being tortured, starving or blocked — can have a love-hate relationship with productivity, fearful that therein lies the road to hackdom. According to Guinness World Records, the most prolific author as measured by words published is the British writer Charles Harold St. John Hamilton (1876-1961), with a whopping 72 to 73 million words to his 25 pen names. But even Hamilton had his limits. In his autobiography, written in the third person (“Like Stendhal,” he declared, he “dislikes the je’s and the moi’s\u200a”), he remembers responding to an “urgent request” by writing an 18,000-word story in one day, an experience he deemed “a tour-de-force that he has never repeated or wanted to repeat.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 265, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 265}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 906, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK/0131-BKS-OPENBOOK-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'More Is More, More, More', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Is More, More, More', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T15:56:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Szalai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Szalai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b5e346d-82db-5dae-8c7d-a74a7a73db62', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b5e346d-82db-5dae-8c7d-a74a7a73db62'}, {'abstract': 'New York Times music critics discuss changes within the genre.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2016/01/29/arts/music/29popcast-pod.html', 'snippet': 'New York Times music critics discuss changes within the genre.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York Times music critics discuss changes within the genre.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: R&B’s New Identity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T15:58:55+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/dd32da1e-14f7-55f2-9b1d-fc8a2b619278', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/dd32da1e-14f7-55f2-9b1d-fc8a2b619278'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: France agrees to return to the Common Market in 1966.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/29/1966-france-returns-to-common-market/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: France agrees to return to the Common Market in 1966.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LUXEMBOURG — The six Common Market nations ended the crisis that has paralyzed their community for seven months. France agreed to return to the Common Market and drop the boycott it began July 1. The first meeting of the revived community at its Brussels headquarters is expected to take place in the second half of February. In a marathon bargaining session that ended early this morning, the ministers from the six nations patched up their differences by resorting to an artful dodge. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1966: France Returns to Common Market', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boycotts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brussels (Belgium)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T16:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0361886-d97b-569c-9985-243a4f04e628', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0361886-d97b-569c-9985-243a4f04e628'}, {'abstract': 'The city of Den Bosch celebrates the 500th anniversary of its native son, Hieronymus Bosch, with a significant exhibition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/travel/a-bosch-retrospective.html', 'snippet': 'The city of Den Bosch celebrates the 500th anniversary of its native son, Hieronymus Bosch, with a significant exhibition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The small Dutch city of ‘s-Hertogenbosch, known as Den Bosch, is celebrating the virtual return of its native son Hieronymus Bosch, who died 500 years ago. Although the city doesn’t own any of the artist’s paintings, this year it will present the largest retrospective of Bosch’s work ever, with all the works on loan from several leading museums. “Jheronimus Bosch – Visions of Genius,” displaying 20 paintings and 19 drawings, runs from Feb. 13 to May 8 at Het Noordbrabants Museum. Seven other museums in the province of North Brabant will present shows.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/travel/31HORIZON/31HORIZON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/travel/31HORIZON/31HORIZON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/travel/31HORIZON/31HORIZON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/travel/31HORIZON/31HORIZON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1345, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/travel/31HORIZON/31HORIZON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/31/travel/31HORIZON/31HORIZON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/travel/31HORIZON/31HORIZON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bosch Retrospective', 'kicker': 'On the Horizon', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art of Bosch <strong> </strong>', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Netherlands', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T16:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'b