firstname': 'Leonard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pitts', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bf0716d-1486-5a8d-a063-cd68f1b374ef', 'word_count': 1232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bf0716d-1486-5a8d-a063-cd68f1b374ef'}, {'abstract': 'A novel pays a call on an aging Thomas Hardy, his young wife and an even younger protégée. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/books/review/winter-by-christopher-nicholson.html', 'snippet': 'A novel pays a call on an aging Thomas Hardy, his young wife and an even younger protégée. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The male giants of English literature have often made bad real-life husbands, and the vindication of their suffering wives has taken various forms. Thomas Carlyle, and posterity, might never have realized what a difficult spouse he was without the diary of Jane Carlyle, which the historian read with dismay after her death. Mary Powell, “Wife to Mr. Milton,” had her youthful ordeal imagined by Robert Graves in a witty first-person novel carrying that title. Now, in “Winter,” the English novelist Christopher Nicholson sets out to avenge (up to a point) Florence Hardy, the \xaddecades-younger second wife of the aged Thomas, as she endures the fall and winter of 1924-25 inside a storm of sickness, jealousy and anger.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31MALLON/31MALLON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 782, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31MALLON/31MALLON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 782}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31MALLON/31MALLON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 787, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31MALLON/31MALLON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1573, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31MALLON/31MALLON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31MALLON/31MALLON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31MALLON/31MALLON-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Winter,’ by Christopher Nicholson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hardy’s Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mallon, Thomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nicholson, Christopher (1956- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Winter (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T16:06:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Mallon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mallon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/befb0a82-d21b-5cb5-b5bf-ead6f6a9f03d', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/befb0a82-d21b-5cb5-b5bf-ead6f6a9f03d'}, {'abstract': 'Posner examines the failings of the federal bench and proposes solutions that law schools might accomplish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/books/review/richard-a-posners-divergent-paths-the-academy-and-the-judiciary.html', 'snippet': 'Posner examines the failings of the federal bench and proposes solutions that law schools might accomplish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Richard Posner did not exist, who would dare invent him? The most-cited legal scholar of all time, who is arguably America’s greatest living judge; a man who publishes a book a year while writing all his own judicial opinions; an icy ration\xadalist who once confessed to unrequited love for his cat. .\u2008.\u2008. It’s all a bit too much to believe. Arthur Conan Doyle’s Sherlock Holmes and Gene Roddenberry’s Mr. Spock are probably the closest anyone has come.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-Roosevelt/0131-BKS-Roosevelt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-Roosevelt/0131-BKS-Roosevelt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-Roosevelt/0131-BKS-Roosevelt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1004, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-Roosevelt/0131-BKS-Roosevelt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1383, 'width': 1411, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 's