'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a625655-7830-51cb-ab09-f3116258c7f9', 'word_count': 1288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a625655-7830-51cb-ab09-f3116258c7f9'}, {'abstract': 'The French auction house Artcurial will hold a sale on May 9 of goods from the restaurant.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/29/la-tour-dargent-paris-dining-temple-to-auction-furnishings-and-more/', 'snippet': 'The French auction house Artcurial will hold a sale on May 9 of goods from the restaurant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — La Tour d’Argent, a Parisian shrine to the art of fine dining that traces its roots to the 16th century, is selling off tableware, furnishings and cooking implements as it seeks to reinvent itself for the 21st century.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'La Tour d’Argent, Paris Dining Temple, to Auction Furnishings and More', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Artcurial', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "La Tour d'Argent (Paris, Restaurant)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T16:10:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia Barbieri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbieri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aef3824f-1581-5f85-9441-379de38ed2b2', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aef3824f-1581-5f85-9441-379de38ed2b2'}, {'abstract': 'The second volume of an authorized biography finds the prime minister at the height of her power. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/books/review/margaret-thatcher-by-charles-moore.html', 'snippet': 'The second volume of an authorized biography finds the prime minister at the height of her power. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'In February 1984, during that period when a succession of decrepit Soviet leaders were dropping like flies, the British prime minister, Margaret Thatcher, traveled to Moscow to attend the funeral of Yuri Andropov. There she made an extremely positive impression on her hosts. Not only did she remain outside in frigid temperatures during the entire ceremony, but her personal security man, who shadowed her in his vast overcoat with immense bulging pockets, drew many admiring glances. “Russian security were deeply impressed by this heavily armed man,” one British official recalled. When, however, Thatcher returned to the Kremlin, she removed her own coat and unzipped her fur-lined boots, “whereupon Detective Superintendent Parker produced a pair of high-heeled shoes from his pockets.” A firearm or a stiletto: Both seemed to have potential in the hands of a leader the Soviets had once christened the “Iron Lady.” But the story is indicative of the way the matter of her gender would often surprise and unsettle Thatcher’s contemporaries.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31ALDOUS/31ALDOUS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31ALDOUS/31ALDOUS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 915}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31ALDOUS/31ALDOUS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 672, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31ALDOUS/31ALDOUS-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1344, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31ALDOUS/31ALDOUS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31ALDOUS/31ALDOUS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/31ALDOUS/31ALDOUS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Margaret Thatcher,’ by Charles Moore', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Everything She Wants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Charles Hilary (1956- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thatcher, Margaret H', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Margaret Thatcher: The Authorized Biography: At Her Zenith: In London, Washington and Moscow (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T16:11:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Aldous', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aldous', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdede516-4464-5631-a2fe-85e9f40c3727', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdede516-4464-5631-a2fe-85e9f40c3727'}, {'abstract': 'This novel’s protagonist, a photographer, spends a year on an island where nature has turned hostile.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/books/review/the-lightkeepers-by-abby-geni.html', 'snippet': 'This novel’s protagonist, a photographer, spends a year on an island where nature has turned hostile.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone who has been traumatized by Alfred Hitchcock’s “The Birds” — a high percentage of its viewers, I’d guess — may hesitate to read beyond the early pages of Abby Geni’s first novel, “The Lightkeepers.” The heroine, a photographer named Miranda, is leaving the island where she has spent the previous year, where the sea gulls now seem to be trying to make sure that no one gets out alive. “A gull slams against Miranda’s temple, knocking her off balance. .\u2008.\u2008. Wings thunder around her shoulders.” But readers who persevere beyond this unnerving beginning will find themselves carried along by a sturdy, rather old-fashioned thriller ramped up by some modern, ecologically themed plot twists.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-Prose/0131-BKS-Prose-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-Prose/0131-BKS-Prose-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/books/review/0131-BKS-Prose/0131-BKS-Prose-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/3