{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Encompass New Opera Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Basile, Frank', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Astronaut's Tale (Opera)", 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T22:43:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aeceba0f-2eec-5b85-8b9e-b71ae63ca7f7', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aeceba0f-2eec-5b85-8b9e-b71ae63ca7f7'}, {'abstract': 'After decades spent as the target of jokes and sneers, the singer’s solo career — and the song that built his legacy — are getting fresh looks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/29/magazine/does-anybody-still-loathe-phil-collins-even-in-the-air-tonight.html', 'snippet': 'After decades spent as the target of jokes and sneers, the singer’s solo career — and the song that built his legacy — are getting fresh looks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For many who were blessed to enter adulthood during the 1980s, it seems as if that decade will hover over us forever, its synth-pop bleats and recycled doomsday sound bites accompanying us into the twilight. Today, that feels truer than ever, as we all welcome back a familiar face: Phil Collins, the amiable sprite of pre-Internet pop. (Both the fairy-tale and computer-graphics definitions of “sprite” apply.) Emerging from semiretirement, Collins is launching a multimonth campaign of remembrance. He’s reissuing eight of his solo albums, including the four blockbusters — “Face Value”; “Hello, I Must Be Going!”; “No Jacket Required”; and “ … But Seriously” — that blanketed the 1980s, selling a combined 24 million copies in the United States alone and generating seven No. 1 singles. He has announced that he’ll record new music and launch a tour. Come October, he’ll publish his autobiography.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/29/magazine/29mag-collins-1/29mag-collins-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 913, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/01/29/magazine/29mag-collins-1/29mag-collins-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 913}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/29/magazine/29mag-collins-1/29mag-collins-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/29/magazine/29mag-collins-1/29mag-collins-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1346, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/29/magazine/29mag-collins-1/29mag-collins-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/01/29/magazine/29mag-collins-1/29mag-collins-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/29/magazine/29mag-collins-1/29mag-collins-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Anybody Still Loathe Phil Collins? (Even ‘In the Air Tonight’?)', 'kicker': 'Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Phil', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Genesis (Music Group)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-29T22:44:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Aaron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aaron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e612081-6ac8-5207-9812-8f2923def042', 'word_count': 2031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e612081-6ac8-5207-9812-8f2923def042'}, {'abstract': 'A compromise to reaffirm concealed-carry reciprocity with 25 states looks like a sellout to gun-control advocates.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/01/29/a-deal-on-concealed-handguns-stirs-virginians/', 'snippet': 'A compromise to reaffirm concealed-carry reciprocity with 25 states looks like a sellout to gun-control advocates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seemed a major victory last month for gun safety advocates: Virginia’s attorney general announced that the state would stop\xa0recognizing concealed-carry gun permits from states with firearm standards rated inferior and unsafe. But it was overturned this week by a compromise between Gov. Terry McAuliffe, a Democrat, and Republican legislative leaders in a surprise deal presented as a public safety advance but denounced as a sellout by angry families of Virginia shooting victims and gun control advocacy groups.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201