: 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-30T19:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adrian Benepe and Merritt Birnbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adrian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benepe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Merritt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Birnbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6d1a803-b566-5c03-820d-d7296206371d', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6d1a803-b566-5c03-820d-d7296206371d'}, {'abstract': 'Our increasing use of Special Operations Forces ensures we never confront the wars fought in our name.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/opinion/sunday/welcome-to-the-age-of-the-commando.html', 'snippet': 'Our increasing use of Special Operations Forces ensures we never confront the wars fought in our name.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A FEW months ago, my wife and I had dinner with a couple we didn’t know very well. It was awkward at first, but there was wine, and conversation soon followed. At one point, the wife asked about my tour in Iraq, where I served four years as a cavalry officer. I began talking about the desert, the tribal politics and the day-to-day travails of counterinsurgency. “That’s all fine,” the husband interrupted. “But tell us about the super-soldiers. The Special-Ops guys. That’s what people care about.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/01/31/opinion/sunday/31gallager/31gallager-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', '