me': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirchner, Cristina Fernandez de', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-31T21:26:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Gilbert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gilbert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5ccdc13-3307-5302-960f-8c865be6923a', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5ccdc13-3307-5302-960f-8c865be6923a'}, {'abstract': 'Only a year ago, he was a favorite to clinch the Republican nomination. But as Iowa voters prepare for their caucuses, he barely registers in the polls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/02/01/us/politics/the-rise-and-fall-of-the-bush-campaign.html', 'snippet': 'Only a year ago, he was a favorite to clinch the Republican nomination. But as Iowa voters prepare for their caucuses, he barely registers in the polls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The biggest stories of this young political year are the surprising surges of the outsider presidential candidates Donald J. Trump and Bernie Sanders, the Democratic senator from Vermont. Close behind is the possible collapse of the once-formidable front-runner, Jeb Bush.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rise and Fall of the Bush Campaign', 'kicker': 'Letter From Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, Jeb', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Right to Rise Policy Solutions Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-31T21:50:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Albert R. Hunt | Bloomberg View', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Albert', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Hunt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/095124fe-3da9-57d6-a341-c544cd99f5e8', 'word_count': 712, 'uri': 'nyt://article/095124fe-3da9-57d6-a341-c544cd99f5e8'}, {'abstract': 'Published in 2011 as a libretto evoking the spy novels Mr. Ashley adored, the work has finally received its world premiere at the Kitchen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/02/01/arts/music/review-quicksand-robert-ashleys-enigmatic-spy-thriller-opera.html', 'snippet': 'Published in 2011 as a libretto evoking the spy novels Mr. Ashley adored, the work has finally received its world premiere at the Kitchen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Ashley is no longer with us, but his voice — calm, mellow, reassuring, quizzical, amused, earnest — lives on. Surrounded by a shifting, unstable electronic haze, it is the only thing you hear at the Kitchen during “Quicksand,” the opera that he wrote in the final years before his death, at 83, in 2014.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01QUICKMUSIC/01QUICKMUSIC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01QUICKMUSIC/01QUICKMUSIC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01QUICKMUSIC/01QUICKMUSIC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01QUICKMUSIC/01QUICKMUSIC-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01QUICKMUSIC/01QUICKMUSIC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01QUICKMUSIC/01QUICKMUSIC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01QUICKMUSIC/01QUICKMUSIC-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Quicksand,’ Robert Ashley’s Enigmatic Spy-Thriller Opera', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '. . . and a Hauntingly Musical Human Voice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ashley, Robert (1930-2014)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paxton, Steve (1939- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Quicksand (Opera)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kitchen, The (Manhattan, NY, Performance Space)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-31T22:01:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0a3991f-2be6-585c-ba27-3b4824593279', 'word_count': 601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0a3991f-2be6-585c-ba27-3b4824593279'}, {'abstract': 'With Congress refusing to close a background check loophole that allows felons to buy guns, Facebook will stop users from placing ads to sell guns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/02/01/opinion/facebooks-sensible-ban-on-gun-ads.html', 'snippet': 'With Congress refusing to close a background check loophole that allows felons to buy guns, Facebook will stop users from placing ads to sell guns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Facebook’s decision last week to prohibit private gun sales on its social networking site and on its photo-sharing app Instagram should make it harder for convicted felons and other dangerous people to acquire deadly weapons. That’s a very good thing, especially considering the unwillingness of lawmakers in Congress to enact sensible gun safety policies.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/opinion/01mon3SUB/01mon3SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/02/01/opinion/01mon3SUB/01mon3SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/opinion/01mon3SUB/01mon3SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/opinion/01mon3SUB/01mon3SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/opinion/01mon3SUB/01mon3SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/02/01/opinion/01mon3SUB/01mon3SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/opinion/01mon3SUB/01mon3SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook’s Sensible Ban on Gun Ads', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Facebook’s Sensible Ban on Gun Ads', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Instagram Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-31T22:08:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/20eb660e-fffd-548c-aad4-3a562bc72f28', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20eb660e-fffd-548c-aad4-3a562bc72f28'}, {'abstract': 'The Dutch-born conductor, who starts leading the New York Philharmonic in 2018, is known for old-school directness and a relentless drive for quality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/02/01/arts/music/jaap-van-zweden-philharmonics-maestro-to-be-sharpens-his-baton.html', 'snippet': 'The Dutch-born conductor, who starts leading the New York Philharmonic in 2018, is known for old-school directness and a relentless drive for quality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DALLAS — It was the morning after he had been named the next music director of the New York Philharmonic, and Jaap van Zweden was already back at work here at his current job, rehearsing the Dallas Symphony Orchestra in Mozart’s Symphony No. 39 with the fastidiousness — and relentlessness — for which he has become known.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01CONDUCTOR/01CONDUCTOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01CONDUCTOR/01CONDUCTOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 635}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01CONDUCTOR/01CONDUCTOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 968, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01CONDUCTOR/01CONDUCTOR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1102, 'width': 1041, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01CONDUCTOR/01CONDUCTOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01CONDUCTOR/01CONDUCTOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '