eature, “Chi-Raq,” Spike Lee tells the story of the epidemic of gun violence in Chicago through the eyes of a gangster’s girlfriend, who persuades women to withhold sex until their menfolk disavow firearms. During production last summer, the film made headlines when the city’s mayor, Rahm Emanuel, criticized the title, which borrowed a nickname Chicagoans had coined for their town’s violent turn.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01SPIKELEE/01SPIKELEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01SPIKELEE/01SPIKELEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01SPIKELEE/01SPIKELEE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01SPIKELEE/01SPIKELEE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1361, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01SPIKELEE/01SPIKELEE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01SPIKELEE/01SPIKELEE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/01/arts/01SPIKELEE/01SPIKELEE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Word With Spike Lee: ‘They Want to Rewind the Clock’', 'kicker': 'The Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Fear, Guns and Oscars Diversity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Spike', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Chi-Raq (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-01-31T22:37:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b1d96b9-d61c-5136-8251-bb62b90286d1', 'word_count': 913, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b1d96b9-d61c-5136-8251-bb62b90286d1'}, {'abstract': 'The future conductor of the New York Philharmonic has a knack for analogies when he rehearses with the Dallas Symphony Orchestra.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/02/01/arts/music/simile-and-metaphor-as-critique-jaap-van-zweden-speaks-to-an-ensemble.html', 'snippet': 'The future conductor of the New York Philharmonic has a knack for analogies when he rehearses with the Dallas Symphony Orchestra.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jaap van Zweden, who will become the music director of the New York Philharmonic in 2018, is known for his intense, demanding rehearsals. Drilling his current ensemble, the Dallas Symphony Orchestra, in a Mozart symphony last week, he used a battery of tools to coax out the sounds he sought: singing out phrases in his pure tenor voice, giving precise technical instructions, and, quite often, venturing into analogies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'c